1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:18,380
Okay. All right.

2
00:00:18,380 --> 00:00:21,040
So we're just going to finish these up and then move on.

3
00:00:22,040 --> 00:00:22,300
All right.

4
00:00:22,360 --> 00:00:27,560
So last regular topic, the winner's curse.

5
00:00:27,560 --> 00:00:30,200
Another example of asymmetric information at work.

6
00:00:30,620 --> 00:00:31,140
All right.

7
00:00:31,200 --> 00:00:34,740
Now, just for a brief review of auction theory,

8
00:00:35,240 --> 00:00:38,280
if you participate in auction websites, you may be able to go

9
00:00:38,280 --> 00:00:41,480
and connect your actual experience to the basics.

10
00:00:42,080 --> 00:00:46,880
But real quick, a first price auction, that's one

11
00:00:46,880 --> 00:00:49,400
where the person who bids the highest wins

12
00:00:49,680 --> 00:00:50,560
and they pay their bid.

13
00:00:50,560 --> 00:00:52,420
All right.

14
00:00:52,420 --> 00:00:53,300
Well, what else can you do?

15
00:00:53,300 --> 00:00:58,760
Well, a second price auction is one where the highest bidder

16
00:00:58,760 --> 00:01:02,760
wins and then they pay the bid of the second highest bidder.

17
00:01:04,640 --> 00:01:07,900
Okay. So, and again, if you think about this a bit more,

18
00:01:07,900 --> 00:01:08,940
you realize, well, let's see.

19
00:01:08,940 --> 00:01:10,140
If we're supposed to have an auction

20
00:01:10,460 --> 00:01:14,360
where they raise the price in pennies or tenths of a penny,

21
00:01:14,780 --> 00:01:17,480
that is for all practical purposes a second price auction,

22
00:01:17,480 --> 00:01:17,740
right?

23
00:01:18,140 --> 00:01:20,300
Because what do you wind up paying?

24
00:01:20,300 --> 00:01:22,420
If they started at a low price

25
00:01:22,420 --> 00:01:25,180
and then raised the price just by a penny every single time,

26
00:01:25,660 --> 00:01:28,500
you wind up paying just a penny above the value

27
00:01:28,500 --> 00:01:29,720
of the second highest bidder

28
00:01:29,720 --> 00:01:32,720
and that's approximately a second price auction.

29
00:01:33,460 --> 00:01:35,700
Right. There's all kinds of other fun auctions.

30
00:01:36,120 --> 00:01:38,660
One of the funnest perhaps is the all pay auction.

31
00:01:39,820 --> 00:01:42,220
That's one where everybody goes and bids

32
00:01:42,500 --> 00:01:44,580
and whatever you bid you have to pay whether you win or lose.

33
00:01:45,560 --> 00:01:47,160
And then the highest bidder wins.

34
00:01:47,160 --> 00:01:48,580
All right.

35
00:01:48,580 --> 00:01:50,380
And if you start thinking about the incentives

36
00:01:50,380 --> 00:01:52,160
in such an auction, if you know that you have

37
00:01:52,160 --> 00:01:53,980
to pay whether you win or not, what do you think that's going

38
00:01:53,980 --> 00:01:54,720
to do to your bids?

39
00:01:55,900 --> 00:01:58,500
Yes. The bids go way down if you know that you have

40
00:01:58,500 --> 00:02:00,120
to pay whether or not you get the object.

41
00:02:00,120 --> 00:02:02,420
So that's, I don't know of any website

42
00:02:02,420 --> 00:02:03,860
that uses the all pay auction.

43
00:02:04,220 --> 00:02:05,500
But, yeah.

44
00:02:05,660 --> 00:02:06,900
How can there be an equilibrium there?

45
00:02:06,900 --> 00:02:10,100
Because if you expect everyone else to put their bid really low,

46
00:02:10,460 --> 00:02:12,760
if you put yours high, then you win, right?

47
00:02:12,760 --> 00:02:14,260
But then you pay your bid.

48
00:02:14,640 --> 00:02:18,420
Yeah. But if everyone, so yes.

49
00:02:18,420 --> 00:02:21,220
Yes. Everyone else is bidding low.

50
00:02:21,220 --> 00:02:24,540
I mean like if you knew exactly what everyone else was bidding,

51
00:02:24,540 --> 00:02:27,160
you'd want to just go a penny above what everyone else is

52
00:02:27,160 --> 00:02:29,460
bidding and then you get the object for a penny more

53
00:02:29,460 --> 00:02:30,260
than everybody else.

54
00:02:30,920 --> 00:02:33,700
Right? But there's no problem with the equilibrium here.

55
00:02:33,940 --> 00:02:35,840
You know, so like we could even do it right now

56
00:02:35,840 --> 00:02:36,420
if we really wanted.

57
00:02:36,420 --> 00:02:38,280
I could auction off a dollar right now.

58
00:02:38,880 --> 00:02:40,920
But with an all pays format

59
00:02:41,280 --> 00:02:43,040
so that whatever you bid you have to give me.

60
00:02:43,700 --> 00:02:45,560
Right? And I've actually known some professors to make money

61
00:02:45,560 --> 00:02:47,780
off of this, but that's out of equilibrium of course.

62
00:02:48,320 --> 00:02:50,620
To be able to make money by auctioning off a dollar

63
00:02:50,620 --> 00:02:51,480
at an all pays auction.

64
00:02:52,040 --> 00:02:54,880
Anyway, so the auctions that we're going to be thinking

65
00:02:54,880 --> 00:02:57,160
about right now are second price auctions.

66
00:02:57,660 --> 00:02:59,480
Right? Again, remember this is one

67
00:02:59,480 --> 00:03:01,520
where the high bidder wins as usual.

68
00:03:01,900 --> 00:03:03,700
But you don't have to pay what you bid.

69
00:03:03,940 --> 00:03:06,240
You only have to pay the bid of the second highest bidder.

70
00:03:07,080 --> 00:03:07,340
All right.

71
00:03:07,640 --> 00:03:09,660
Now, so imagine we've got a second price auction

72
00:03:09,660 --> 00:03:10,980
where we've got N participants.

73
00:03:11,600 --> 00:03:12,720
Right? So we've got N bidders.

74
00:03:13,320 --> 00:03:14,880
Right? And then further imagine

75
00:03:14,880 --> 00:03:17,220
that every single bidder has rational expectations.

76
00:03:17,700 --> 00:03:19,740
Right? So everybody's got rational expectations.

77
00:03:19,740 --> 00:03:23,020
So everyone estimates the value of the object.

78
00:03:23,620 --> 00:03:26,040
Right? As being the value that you estimate is going to be

79
00:03:26,040 --> 00:03:26,980
as your personal VI.

80
00:03:26,980 --> 00:03:28,580
So that's the value you estimate.

81
00:03:29,000 --> 00:03:30,700
Will be equal to V, the truth.

82
00:03:31,400 --> 00:03:32,400
That's the true value.

83
00:03:32,860 --> 00:03:35,520
Plus a random error term epsilon I.

84
00:03:35,520 --> 00:03:38,400
And then epsilon I is just normally distributed

85
00:03:38,400 --> 00:03:40,660
with mean zero and variance sigma squared.

86
00:03:41,620 --> 00:03:41,800
All right.

87
00:03:42,140 --> 00:03:44,200
Now notice, since you have rational expectations,

88
00:03:44,200 --> 00:03:45,440
your estimate is unbiased.

89
00:03:45,920 --> 00:03:48,760
On average you are neither too high nor too low.

90
00:03:48,760 --> 00:03:51,740
And it seems very intuitive to almost everyone

91
00:03:51,740 --> 00:03:53,880
that you should just go and bid your true valuation.

92
00:03:54,340 --> 00:03:58,100
It seems intuitive to just bid your true valuation.

93
00:03:58,420 --> 00:04:00,340
In fact, if you think about it, this seems

94
00:04:00,340 --> 00:04:01,680
like a weakly dominant strategy.

95
00:04:01,680 --> 00:04:02,560
And on homework you will get

96
00:04:02,560 --> 00:04:04,320
to prove it's a weakly dominant strategy.

97
00:04:04,700 --> 00:04:06,660
It's like why would that be weakly dominant?

98
00:04:06,660 --> 00:04:08,260
Well, just to give you the quick version.

99
00:04:08,680 --> 00:04:11,220
But to not deprive you of the fun of proving it to yourself.

100
00:04:11,860 --> 00:04:14,140
Remember, so in a second price auction, you know,

101
00:04:14,140 --> 00:04:16,140
if you bid, suppose that you have a really high bid

102
00:04:16,140 --> 00:04:20,200
and you bid the true amount, does that mean that you're going

103
00:04:20,200 --> 00:04:21,400
to have to pay the really high amount?

104
00:04:22,780 --> 00:04:24,120
No, you only pay the really high amount

105
00:04:24,200 --> 00:04:26,660
if the second highest bidder also has

106
00:04:26,660 --> 00:04:27,380
that really high amount.

107
00:04:28,000 --> 00:04:30,520
Now if that is actually on average correct,

108
00:04:31,060 --> 00:04:33,820
you don't want to shave your bid because either it won't matter,

109
00:04:33,820 --> 00:04:35,900
in which case you still pay the bid of the second highest guy.

110
00:04:36,780 --> 00:04:38,200
Or it does matter.

111
00:04:38,580 --> 00:04:41,420
And so if shaving your bid does actually matter,

112
00:04:41,600 --> 00:04:42,460
what just happened to you?

113
00:04:42,460 --> 00:04:45,020
You lost the auction.

114
00:04:45,020 --> 00:04:48,000
Even though you valued it at more than you'd be paying.

115
00:04:48,340 --> 00:04:49,480
So that's not a bad outcome.

116
00:04:50,180 --> 00:04:50,440
All right.

117
00:04:51,320 --> 00:04:56,360
So, but, but, when, given the way

118
00:04:56,360 --> 00:04:57,540
that we have set this problem up,

119
00:04:57,920 --> 00:05:00,940
bidding your true value will actually be a disaster

120
00:05:01,020 --> 00:05:01,700
on average.

121
00:05:02,280 --> 00:05:05,740
Why? Because if the average error is zero

122
00:05:06,060 --> 00:05:08,640
and everyone sees the truth in the ads on an average error,

123
00:05:08,780 --> 00:05:09,780
who's going to win the auction?

124
00:05:09,780 --> 00:05:13,880
Yes, the one with the biggest error.

125
00:05:13,880 --> 00:05:15,600
Right? The one, and particularly the one

126
00:05:15,600 --> 00:05:17,140
with the most positive error.

127
00:05:17,620 --> 00:05:20,280
So every single person in the room gets a random, you know,

128
00:05:20,280 --> 00:05:22,280
you all get a random error, right?

129
00:05:22,280 --> 00:05:23,620
And then I go and auction it off.

130
00:05:23,620 --> 00:05:26,120
Everyone believes the truth plus a random error.

131
00:05:27,120 --> 00:05:28,220
But then there's a little problem.

132
00:05:28,220 --> 00:05:30,820
The person who wins is going to be the one

133
00:05:30,820 --> 00:05:32,900
who has made the biggest positive mistake.

134
00:05:33,740 --> 00:05:36,640
And conditional upon winning is your average error zero.

135
00:05:37,520 --> 00:05:39,260
Conditional on winning, you probably, you should expect

136
00:05:39,260 --> 00:05:40,640
to have a big positive error.

137
00:05:41,060 --> 00:05:42,760
You can imagine there were, that is a million.

138
00:05:42,760 --> 00:05:45,840
In that case, my God, who, like what is the winner going

139
00:05:45,840 --> 00:05:48,520
to be, someone who's a one in a million overestimate

140
00:05:49,000 --> 00:05:49,640
of the value.

141
00:05:50,200 --> 00:05:52,700
And you go and bid that, and then what do you pay?

142
00:05:52,960 --> 00:05:55,460
You bid the two in a million overestimate.

143
00:05:55,620 --> 00:05:57,320
Or rather you pay the two in a million overestimate

144
00:05:57,560 --> 00:05:59,340
because you just have to pay whatever the second highest

145
00:05:59,340 --> 00:05:59,780
bidder paid.

146
00:06:00,680 --> 00:06:02,400
All right, so the average estimate is zero.

147
00:06:02,640 --> 00:06:04,440
The average winning estimate is positive.

148
00:06:04,740 --> 00:06:07,280
So given that you win, you can expect

149
00:06:07,280 --> 00:06:09,240
to have overestimated the item's true value.

150
00:06:09,740 --> 00:06:12,020
Of course, it's possible that just by sheer coincidence,

151
00:06:12,020 --> 00:06:14,080
everyone will overestimate and then there's no problem.

152
00:06:14,600 --> 00:06:16,380
But that's highly unlikely, of course,

153
00:06:16,380 --> 00:06:17,900
that everyone will overestimate.

154
00:06:18,900 --> 00:06:21,240
All right, now this problem has a very special name.

155
00:06:21,240 --> 00:06:22,820
It's called the winner's curse.

156
00:06:23,480 --> 00:06:24,700
The winner's curse.

157
00:06:27,700 --> 00:06:29,860
And the more serious your error,

158
00:06:29,860 --> 00:06:31,840
the more likely you are to win, right?

159
00:06:31,840 --> 00:06:34,140
And the more likely you are to win, the more likely you are

160
00:06:34,140 --> 00:06:35,460
to have made a serious error.

161
00:06:35,980 --> 00:06:38,900
And remember, winning at an auction can be really bad.

162
00:06:38,900 --> 00:06:41,200
So true story.

163
00:06:41,660 --> 00:06:44,520
So you may have heard that Orly Ashenfelter at Princeton,

164
00:06:44,520 --> 00:06:49,480
I think he founded the journal, like, not Winonomics,

165
00:06:49,480 --> 00:06:50,900
it's like, I don't remember what it is.

166
00:06:51,280 --> 00:06:53,260
But anyway, it's like the first big journal

167
00:06:53,260 --> 00:06:55,100
in the economics of wine, right?

168
00:06:55,100 --> 00:06:57,120
And also, this is back when David Carver was there,

169
00:06:57,120 --> 00:06:58,660
he's also a big wine collector.

170
00:06:59,520 --> 00:07:01,560
There was, this is back in the old days

171
00:07:01,960 --> 00:07:04,140
when internet bidding is still very novel.

172
00:07:04,640 --> 00:07:06,280
So anyway, there's a big wine auction.

173
00:07:06,280 --> 00:07:09,680
What they would do every year is fax in a list

174
00:07:09,680 --> 00:07:12,160
of super lowball bids on every bottle of wine,

175
00:07:12,960 --> 00:07:15,160
figuring that they would then go and win a couple bottles

176
00:07:15,160 --> 00:07:15,980
at bargain prices.

177
00:07:16,580 --> 00:07:17,920
But that one year, there was,

178
00:07:17,920 --> 00:07:20,840
for some reason the live auction was greatly disrupted.

179
00:07:21,300 --> 00:07:23,700
So they were not able to hold it for some reason.

180
00:07:23,700 --> 00:07:25,600
I don't know whether it was an earthquake.

181
00:07:25,600 --> 00:07:26,640
I don't remember what happened.

182
00:07:26,640 --> 00:07:28,480
But anyway, the regular in-person auction,

183
00:07:28,480 --> 00:07:31,460
which is 95% of the bids, didn't happen.

184
00:07:31,800 --> 00:07:34,880
And then under state law, they still had to go

185
00:07:34,880 --> 00:07:36,980
and give the wine to whoever it was who sent

186
00:07:36,980 --> 00:07:38,380
in the highest bid they received.

187
00:07:38,840 --> 00:07:41,840
So Ashenfelter and Card wind up getting like 100

188
00:07:41,840 --> 00:07:44,700
or 1,000 bottles of wine, all at bargain prices.

189
00:07:44,700 --> 00:07:46,700
But they didn't want that much wine.

190
00:07:47,380 --> 00:07:50,160
They won by accident, right, which was,

191
00:07:50,160 --> 00:07:52,260
and it was a problem, right?

192
00:07:52,260 --> 00:07:53,660
An auction is not the kind of thing

193
00:07:53,660 --> 00:07:55,060
that you necessarily want to win.

194
00:07:55,060 --> 00:07:56,780
You only want to win if you're getting a good deal.

195
00:07:56,780 --> 00:07:59,800
And they had no idea that there would be any possibility

196
00:07:59,800 --> 00:08:02,260
that they would be capturing most of the bottles

197
00:08:02,260 --> 00:08:03,260
that were auctioned that day.

198
00:08:03,260 --> 00:08:04,520
And yet they did.

199
00:08:05,480 --> 00:08:07,880
Okay. All right.

200
00:08:08,240 --> 00:08:09,860
So this is an odd strategic situation.

201
00:08:09,860 --> 00:08:11,080
Now, what can you do about it?

202
00:08:11,660 --> 00:08:14,500
Well, so suppose that you know all the VIs.

203
00:08:15,080 --> 00:08:16,600
So suppose that you know your VI,

204
00:08:16,600 --> 00:08:18,440
but you also know everybody else's.

205
00:08:18,840 --> 00:08:20,400
Then there's a really obvious solution.

206
00:08:22,020 --> 00:08:23,600
So, and what would that be?

207
00:08:24,640 --> 00:08:25,720
Take an average, right?

208
00:08:25,720 --> 00:08:27,400
Because your error, there's nothing special

209
00:08:27,400 --> 00:08:28,860
about what happens to Bill Adams

210
00:08:28,860 --> 00:08:30,360
that seems likely to be true to you.

211
00:08:30,660 --> 00:08:32,700
You want to go and get an estimate that's likely to be true

212
00:08:32,700 --> 00:08:37,240
to everyone, and then you are, you just go and take them,

213
00:08:37,240 --> 00:08:38,540
sum them, take the average,

214
00:08:38,540 --> 00:08:41,640
and then you actually will be very, or will,

215
00:08:41,640 --> 00:08:44,600
with a large N, be very close to the true value.

216
00:08:45,320 --> 00:08:45,560
All right.

217
00:08:45,560 --> 00:08:47,840
So that would be a very good approach.

218
00:08:48,160 --> 00:08:50,860
Now, if you're thinking, well, this doesn't make sense to me

219
00:08:50,860 --> 00:08:52,880
because people just have different values.

220
00:08:53,380 --> 00:08:55,560
The winner's auction, or the winner's curtsy,

221
00:08:55,560 --> 00:08:57,240
or the best thing of it is something like bidding

222
00:08:57,240 --> 00:09:00,280
over an oil well, or oil fields,

223
00:09:00,280 --> 00:09:03,260
where there is a common value to anyone on earth

224
00:09:03,260 --> 00:09:05,920
who buys it is basically going to be worth the value

225
00:09:05,920 --> 00:09:07,580
of the oil minus the extraction cost.

226
00:09:08,260 --> 00:09:08,380
All right.

227
00:09:08,380 --> 00:09:10,720
So, you know, the idea of the winner's curse is

228
00:09:10,720 --> 00:09:13,380
when there's actually some true thing in the world

229
00:09:13,380 --> 00:09:14,320
that you're trying to estimate.

230
00:09:14,720 --> 00:09:16,240
That's when the winner's curse comes into play.

231
00:09:16,660 --> 00:09:19,740
If, in fact, what we call random error is just your taste,

232
00:09:20,280 --> 00:09:22,980
then there's no problem again, because then if you happen

233
00:09:22,980 --> 00:09:25,600
to be the person who just loves this thing more than anyone else

234
00:09:25,600 --> 00:09:27,360
on earth, then, of course, you want to go

235
00:09:27,360 --> 00:09:29,500
and bid your true value, and the fact that you win,

236
00:09:29,500 --> 00:09:31,920
all that means is that you happen

237
00:09:31,920 --> 00:09:34,700
to really like this thing, which is not a problem, right?

238
00:09:34,700 --> 00:09:36,880
It's not a problem to like something more

239
00:09:36,880 --> 00:09:40,620
than everybody else, but it is a problem to be trying to,

240
00:09:40,620 --> 00:09:41,960
to be bidding on something

241
00:09:42,200 --> 00:09:45,000
for its actual regular market value,

242
00:09:45,180 --> 00:09:46,600
and then to have a false belief

243
00:09:46,600 --> 00:09:48,040
about what that market value is.

244
00:09:48,560 --> 00:09:50,560
So, that is the key distinction of mind.

245
00:09:50,560 --> 00:09:51,760
All right.

246
00:09:51,760 --> 00:09:53,420
So, anyway, all of these are common knowledge

247
00:09:53,420 --> 00:09:55,100
that it's really easy to solve the winner's curse,

248
00:09:55,100 --> 00:09:59,220
but even if you only know VI, there's another solution.

249
00:09:59,840 --> 00:10:00,640
So, remember I was saying,

250
00:10:00,640 --> 00:10:01,900
what do you do in the all-pay auction?

251
00:10:02,900 --> 00:10:03,240
You?

252
00:10:03,740 --> 00:10:04,260
Underbid.

253
00:10:04,260 --> 00:10:07,180
Underbid, because if everyone bid their true valuation

254
00:10:07,180 --> 00:10:08,140
in an all-pay auction,

255
00:10:08,420 --> 00:10:10,480
typical person just loses whatever amount they bid.

256
00:10:10,480 --> 00:10:12,440
That doesn't sound like a very good deal.

257
00:10:13,140 --> 00:10:13,460
All right.

258
00:10:13,740 --> 00:10:16,860
So, similarly, in a winner's curse situation,

259
00:10:16,860 --> 00:10:18,780
the sensible thing to do is to bid less

260
00:10:19,280 --> 00:10:20,440
in your true valuation.

261
00:10:20,440 --> 00:10:24,680
That way, if you do win, you'll want to win,

262
00:10:25,180 --> 00:10:27,900
because you have put your bid down so low

263
00:10:28,400 --> 00:10:31,480
that actually now winning is a good deal.

264
00:10:31,480 --> 00:10:32,780
All right.

265
00:10:32,780 --> 00:10:34,580
So, in other words, if you find

266
00:10:34,580 --> 00:10:37,080
out that the winner normally overestimates by 80%,

267
00:10:37,580 --> 00:10:39,820
then you only want to bid the reciprocal of that.

268
00:10:39,820 --> 00:10:41,420
So, actually, I think that's so, yeah.

269
00:10:41,420 --> 00:10:43,740
So, then you want to bid, so underbid.

270
00:10:43,740 --> 00:10:45,080
Actually, I wrote 80%.

271
00:10:45,080 --> 00:10:46,320
I think it's actually 83, right,

272
00:10:46,320 --> 00:10:49,420
because if the winner usually bids 6 5ths of the true value,

273
00:10:49,420 --> 00:10:52,460
then you'd want to go and bid 5 6ths of the true value.

274
00:10:53,080 --> 00:10:54,680
Right. And that way, if you win, you don't get burned.

275
00:10:55,760 --> 00:10:58,060
Right. Now, a funny application of the winner's curse.

276
00:10:58,620 --> 00:11:01,760
Now, for things like moral hazard, as I was mentioning,

277
00:11:01,760 --> 00:11:03,000
these things have been known to people

278
00:11:03,000 --> 00:11:04,440
in industry for centuries.

279
00:11:04,440 --> 00:11:05,960
It doesn't require any brilliance.

280
00:11:06,400 --> 00:11:09,140
It's not like economists really figured anything out here.

281
00:11:09,860 --> 00:11:11,580
But the winner's curse, actually,

282
00:11:11,580 --> 00:11:13,580
economists did seem to figure something out.

283
00:11:14,360 --> 00:11:16,900
How so? Well, when economists started looking

284
00:11:16,900 --> 00:11:18,780
for empirical applications of the winner's curse,

285
00:11:18,780 --> 00:11:21,060
one thing they did is investigate things

286
00:11:21,060 --> 00:11:23,060
like bidding on the value of oil fields.

287
00:11:23,580 --> 00:11:25,520
And here was something that they often noticed.

288
00:11:25,880 --> 00:11:28,060
You go and you get the geologist's report,

289
00:11:28,060 --> 00:11:29,940
and then they go and calculate and put in the world price

290
00:11:29,940 --> 00:11:31,260
of oil, blah, blah, blah.

291
00:11:31,580 --> 00:11:34,020
I'll go through all the pages of math, and then you get

292
00:11:34,020 --> 00:11:36,300
to the second to last line, and then they'll just be

293
00:11:36,300 --> 00:11:39,420
like minus 40%, and then they come up with the bid.

294
00:11:40,160 --> 00:11:41,100
And when the economist said, well,

295
00:11:41,100 --> 00:11:44,280
why do you subtract 40% off your estimate?

296
00:11:44,740 --> 00:11:45,920
And all they could say is, well,

297
00:11:45,920 --> 00:11:46,980
if you don't do that, you lose money.

298
00:11:46,980 --> 00:11:49,540
But they didn't actually seem to have figured

299
00:11:49,540 --> 00:11:50,620
out what the problem was.

300
00:11:51,040 --> 00:11:53,180
All they had was a rule of thumb that allowed them

301
00:11:53,180 --> 00:11:54,040
to avoid losing money.

302
00:11:55,140 --> 00:11:56,320
So there I would say

303
00:11:56,320 --> 00:11:58,460
that economists actually really did add some insight,

304
00:11:58,900 --> 00:12:00,460
where they actually were able to come up with a theory

305
00:12:00,460 --> 00:12:05,460
that explained what was going wrong with the seemingly correct

306
00:12:05,460 --> 00:12:07,620
approach of just getting the best geologist,

307
00:12:07,620 --> 00:12:09,400
having them find out the amount of money, and then bidding that.

308
00:12:09,940 --> 00:12:12,660
So anyway, here is a victory for economics.

309
00:12:12,660 --> 00:12:17,140
We're at least able to rationalize a major existing

310
00:12:17,140 --> 00:12:20,160
business practice that could not be rationalized

311
00:12:20,160 --> 00:12:22,220
by the people doing it, other than just to say it works.

312
00:12:23,500 --> 00:12:24,700
So, kind of cool.

313
00:12:26,300 --> 00:12:27,040
All right, questions?

314
00:12:27,480 --> 00:12:28,000
Questions?

315
00:12:29,380 --> 00:12:31,540
So I think I actually assigned Landsberg's chapter

316
00:12:31,540 --> 00:12:32,440
on the winner's curse.

317
00:12:32,840 --> 00:12:36,280
He actually, I think he actually understands all this perfectly

318
00:12:36,280 --> 00:12:38,160
well, but he tries to get a little bit cute

319
00:12:38,160 --> 00:12:40,640
and say the very fact that you're reading this book shows

320
00:12:40,640 --> 00:12:44,480
you probably overestimated its value, but that would only be

321
00:12:44,480 --> 00:12:47,740
true if you hadn't done the proper discounting.

322
00:12:48,200 --> 00:12:51,160
So, basically, like if you really understood the model,

323
00:12:51,160 --> 00:12:53,740
then you'd be about to reach for a book, and then say, well,

324
00:12:54,020 --> 00:12:56,000
the very fact that I'm about to reach for it suggests

325
00:12:56,000 --> 00:12:57,200
that I probably overestimated,

326
00:12:57,760 --> 00:12:59,560
and then you would do the adjustment, all right,

327
00:12:59,560 --> 00:13:02,160
so subtract off 20%, 30%, whatever,

328
00:13:02,160 --> 00:13:03,340
and then if you still pick it up,

329
00:13:03,340 --> 00:13:04,900
then on average you should get the benefits.

330
00:13:05,920 --> 00:13:07,040
So that is the idea.

331
00:13:07,040 --> 00:13:11,680
All right, so questions about the winner's curse?

332
00:13:12,300 --> 00:13:13,500
Right, and as you can see,

333
00:13:13,500 --> 00:13:15,060
this does involve asymmetric information

334
00:13:15,060 --> 00:13:17,320
because it can be solved very easily

335
00:13:17,320 --> 00:13:19,700
if everybody knows exactly what everybody thinks.

336
00:13:20,400 --> 00:13:22,260
Then there's no longer any winner's curse problem.

337
00:13:23,000 --> 00:13:23,100
Yes?

338
00:13:23,100 --> 00:13:23,200
Yes?

339
00:13:23,200 --> 00:13:27,000
For bidding on the dollar bill, what's the error there?

340
00:13:27,800 --> 00:13:29,860
Ah, so bidding on a dollar bill, yes,

341
00:13:29,860 --> 00:13:31,120
what would be the error on that?

342
00:13:31,660 --> 00:13:34,900
So, maybe not understanding what's happening.

343
00:13:34,900 --> 00:13:38,500
I mean, it's a weird auction, like, let's bid on a dollar bill.

344
00:13:38,500 --> 00:13:40,160
I can easily see a lot of people saying, what?

345
00:13:40,160 --> 00:13:41,960
What do you mean bid on a dollar bill?

346
00:13:42,160 --> 00:13:44,900
You can't bid on money, right?

347
00:13:44,900 --> 00:13:46,120
So if you're confused by it,

348
00:13:46,120 --> 00:13:47,920
that could lead to some error, for example.

349
00:13:48,980 --> 00:13:52,740
You know, there's also just people accidentally taking the

350
00:13:52,740 --> 00:13:54,600
wrong money out of their wallet or opening up their mouth

351
00:13:54,600 --> 00:13:56,360
and saying the wrong thing, right?

352
00:13:56,360 --> 00:13:58,860
How many of you, have you ever been thinking, okay,

353
00:13:58,860 --> 00:14:00,380
don't say the wrong thing, don't say the wrong thing,

354
00:14:00,380 --> 00:14:01,980
and then you open up your mouth and say it anyway?

355
00:14:01,980 --> 00:14:05,640
It's like last week's speaker, the day before, okay,

356
00:14:05,640 --> 00:14:08,220
don't call him Mark Bills, who's an economist

357
00:14:08,220 --> 00:14:08,940
and a totally different guy.

358
00:14:08,940 --> 00:14:12,400
And then I'm introducing new people, and then I say, oh,

359
00:14:12,400 --> 00:14:13,940
everyone meet Mark Bills.

360
00:14:14,380 --> 00:14:15,340
It's actually David Bills.

361
00:14:15,660 --> 00:14:17,480
Oh, my God, it's kind of embarrassing.

362
00:14:17,480 --> 00:14:19,440
It makes it sound like I invited you to come out here,

363
00:14:19,440 --> 00:14:20,240
and I don't know your name.

364
00:14:20,400 --> 00:14:22,960
And if I hadn't thought about it,

365
00:14:22,960 --> 00:14:24,620
maybe I would actually have gotten it right.

366
00:14:24,700 --> 00:14:29,000
But, yeah, so, anyway, he was very gentlemanly about it.

367
00:14:29,000 --> 00:14:30,320
It happens all the time, sure.

368
00:14:30,320 --> 00:14:32,640
People just invite you out and forget your name,

369
00:14:32,640 --> 00:14:34,140
mix you up with other people.

370
00:14:34,140 --> 00:14:34,280
Yes?

371
00:14:34,280 --> 00:14:43,200
I happily pay $3 to win a dollar in an auction sometimes.

372
00:14:43,580 --> 00:14:44,500
You're the winner.

373
00:14:44,660 --> 00:14:45,960
No one wants to be a loser.

374
00:14:46,460 --> 00:14:48,560
Right. Yeah, there's something to that.

375
00:14:48,660 --> 00:14:51,120
And actually, let's see, I don't think we're going to talk

376
00:14:51,120 --> 00:14:53,400
about this specifically, but that is, so, you know,

377
00:14:53,400 --> 00:14:55,500
we're moving into psychology and economics very soon,

378
00:14:55,500 --> 00:14:59,260
and that is a general observation that I think deserves

379
00:14:59,260 --> 00:15:02,180
to be studied more, which is that people don't like losing.

380
00:15:02,860 --> 00:15:06,400
Even if losers are well-paid, there's something bitter

381
00:15:06,400 --> 00:15:10,760
about losing, which causes people to resist.

382
00:15:11,240 --> 00:15:13,460
So, you know, like we were just going over return to education.

383
00:15:13,960 --> 00:15:15,420
So, you suppose you convince people

384
00:15:15,420 --> 00:15:17,020
that it's still financially very rewarding

385
00:15:17,020 --> 00:15:19,360
to be a D student and just finish.

386
00:15:19,360 --> 00:15:22,020
And look, D student still makes a lot more than an F student,

387
00:15:22,020 --> 00:15:23,060
so just finish the degree.

388
00:15:23,760 --> 00:15:25,720
Right. And yet, what is it like to have to go

389
00:15:25,720 --> 00:15:27,140
through four years of receiving D's?

390
00:15:27,140 --> 00:15:29,200
It's unpleasant.

391
00:15:31,000 --> 00:15:31,260
All right.

392
00:15:31,560 --> 00:15:33,460
So, last topic, efficiency implications

393
00:15:33,460 --> 00:15:34,380
of asymmetric information.

394
00:15:34,940 --> 00:15:36,260
So, like we already said,

395
00:15:36,700 --> 00:15:38,620
if you ever had an undergraduate class

396
00:15:38,620 --> 00:15:40,720
or even a high school class where you were taught

397
00:15:41,220 --> 00:15:43,260
that imperfect information implies market failure,

398
00:15:43,420 --> 00:15:44,440
you were taught incorrectly.

399
00:15:45,080 --> 00:15:47,320
It is not true that imperfect information implies

400
00:15:47,320 --> 00:15:47,980
market failure.

401
00:15:48,480 --> 00:15:50,860
Say imperfect information implies the possibility

402
00:15:51,100 --> 00:15:54,000
of market failure, but implying something

403
00:15:54,000 --> 00:15:57,980
and applying a possibility are two radically different things.

404
00:15:57,980 --> 00:15:59,420
Right. It's possible there's a clown

405
00:15:59,420 --> 00:16:01,960
in the room is a very different result

406
00:16:01,960 --> 00:16:03,580
than there is a clown in the room.

407
00:16:03,580 --> 00:16:05,860
You know, it's sort of possible there's a clown in the room,

408
00:16:06,100 --> 00:16:08,920
but I wouldn't get all worked up over it.

409
00:16:08,920 --> 00:16:10,560
It's just a mere possibility.

410
00:16:10,720 --> 00:16:12,660
Maybe he's hiding under a desk somewhere.

411
00:16:13,140 --> 00:16:13,400
All right.

412
00:16:13,400 --> 00:16:14,460
But so, all right, fine.

413
00:16:14,460 --> 00:16:15,160
It's possible.

414
00:16:15,640 --> 00:16:16,220
Who cares?

415
00:16:16,500 --> 00:16:17,620
But on the other hand, if there really,

416
00:16:17,620 --> 00:16:18,840
if we knew there were a clown in the room,

417
00:16:18,840 --> 00:16:19,800
now that would be interesting.

418
00:16:19,800 --> 00:16:23,880
And same for many other possibilities

419
00:16:23,880 --> 00:16:24,900
versus actualities.

420
00:16:25,340 --> 00:16:25,500
All right.

421
00:16:25,500 --> 00:16:27,040
So anyway, it is not true

422
00:16:27,040 --> 00:16:28,980
that imperfect information implies market failure.

423
00:16:29,260 --> 00:16:31,320
You say imperfect information implies a possibility.

424
00:16:32,040 --> 00:16:33,020
Very different conclusion.

425
00:16:33,480 --> 00:16:34,560
Now, as we've already seen,

426
00:16:34,560 --> 00:16:37,800
symmetric imperfect information does not imply,

427
00:16:37,800 --> 00:16:40,300
it does not imply market failure.

428
00:16:41,040 --> 00:16:41,820
Right. In fact, you see,

429
00:16:41,820 --> 00:16:44,380
symmetric imperfect information implies no market failure,

430
00:16:44,700 --> 00:16:45,640
is a better way of putting it.

431
00:16:46,020 --> 00:16:47,560
There's no market failure caused

432
00:16:47,560 --> 00:16:48,960
by symmetric imperfect information.

433
00:16:48,960 --> 00:16:52,180
If everyone is equally in the dark, then the idea

434
00:16:52,180 --> 00:16:57,620
that it's just a matter of information is a scarce good,

435
00:16:57,620 --> 00:16:58,820
it's no different from anything else,

436
00:16:59,000 --> 00:17:01,080
that whole argument goes through perfectly.

437
00:17:01,920 --> 00:17:02,180
All right.

438
00:17:02,760 --> 00:17:04,940
Now, if all, now what about the case

439
00:17:04,940 --> 00:17:07,080
where all market agents are equally informed,

440
00:17:07,460 --> 00:17:08,700
but the government knows more?

441
00:17:09,440 --> 00:17:12,100
So is that symmetric or asymmetric information?

442
00:17:12,980 --> 00:17:13,360
Asymmetric.

443
00:17:13,580 --> 00:17:15,460
Asymmetric, because somebody,

444
00:17:15,940 --> 00:17:17,980
somebody on earth knows more than other people.

445
00:17:17,980 --> 00:17:19,780
Even if it's just one organization.

446
00:17:19,780 --> 00:17:21,940
So everybody is equal except the government,

447
00:17:22,380 --> 00:17:23,660
which is more equal than others.

448
00:17:23,660 --> 00:17:25,200
I mean, knows more than everybody else.

449
00:17:25,840 --> 00:17:28,820
But in that case, there is a very simple way

450
00:17:28,820 --> 00:17:30,220
to solve this market failure.

451
00:17:30,220 --> 00:17:32,720
The government's the only organization

452
00:17:32,720 --> 00:17:33,700
that knows better than others.

453
00:17:33,780 --> 00:17:35,300
Everybody else is equally in the dark.

454
00:17:35,680 --> 00:17:38,820
What very light-handed measure can the government take

455
00:17:38,820 --> 00:17:39,540
to solve the problem?

456
00:17:39,820 --> 00:17:41,600
Well, if I were a bureaucrat, I'd drop out of the government

457
00:17:41,600 --> 00:17:43,060
and use my knowledge to make a lot of profit.

458
00:17:43,320 --> 00:17:45,580
Right. There is that, although that does not

459
00:17:45,580 --> 00:17:46,580
by itself solve the market,

460
00:17:46,580 --> 00:17:48,500
and it does not solve the market failure actually.

461
00:17:48,720 --> 00:17:50,260
Then every time someone offers you a deal, it's like,

462
00:17:50,260 --> 00:17:51,540
are you that guy who quit the government

463
00:17:51,540 --> 00:17:52,980
and is trying to make money off of this?

464
00:17:53,380 --> 00:17:54,560
Maybe I don't want to sell to you.

465
00:17:55,600 --> 00:17:55,860
All right.

466
00:17:56,280 --> 00:17:58,380
So again, public interest in the government.

467
00:17:58,380 --> 00:17:59,880
They want to solve market failures

468
00:17:59,880 --> 00:18:01,220
in the cheapest possible way.

469
00:18:01,220 --> 00:18:01,980
They know stuff.

470
00:18:02,100 --> 00:18:03,080
Other people don't know.

471
00:18:03,080 --> 00:18:03,680
What can they do?

472
00:18:03,940 --> 00:18:05,760
Tell people.

473
00:18:06,080 --> 00:18:07,160
Hello, everybody.

474
00:18:07,260 --> 00:18:08,620
Opioids are dangerous.

475
00:18:08,620 --> 00:18:11,880
Other people don't know this.

476
00:18:11,880 --> 00:18:13,900
You're all equally in the dark, thinking it's safe.

477
00:18:13,900 --> 00:18:15,460
But the government scientists have figured

478
00:18:15,460 --> 00:18:18,420
out they're dangerous, so everybody listen up.

479
00:18:18,820 --> 00:18:19,960
They're dangerous.

480
00:18:20,560 --> 00:18:21,920
Here's how dangerous.

481
00:18:22,240 --> 00:18:23,400
Okay. Our job's done.

482
00:18:23,520 --> 00:18:23,820
All right.

483
00:18:23,820 --> 00:18:24,300
That's it.

484
00:18:24,300 --> 00:18:25,420
Then there's no more drugs.

485
00:18:25,420 --> 00:18:28,080
All that you have is a big government announcement.

486
00:18:28,860 --> 00:18:31,680
If new people, new ignorant people are coming along all

487
00:18:31,680 --> 00:18:33,720
the time, then you could periodically announce it.

488
00:18:34,100 --> 00:18:36,260
Just want to let everyone know, or you can imagine you're

489
00:18:36,260 --> 00:18:38,160
like at a wedding, and the government takes a fork

490
00:18:38,160 --> 00:18:39,660
and starts digging the champagne glass.

491
00:18:39,900 --> 00:18:40,960
Ding, ding, ding, ding, ding.

492
00:18:41,200 --> 00:18:42,920
Opioids are dangerous, people.

493
00:18:43,380 --> 00:18:44,520
Just thought you ought to know.

494
00:18:44,520 --> 00:18:45,480
All right.

495
00:18:45,480 --> 00:18:48,240
So that is the extent of the remedy.

496
00:18:49,240 --> 00:18:49,420
All right.

497
00:18:49,420 --> 00:18:52,480
Now, of course, governments do this kind of thing

498
00:18:52,480 --> 00:18:54,480
with public service campaigns and so on,

499
00:18:54,480 --> 00:18:57,500
as well as public education curricula.

500
00:18:57,900 --> 00:19:00,300
But very rarely do governments limit themselves

501
00:19:00,300 --> 00:19:03,380
to just saying, you-hoo, I've got some information.

502
00:19:03,640 --> 00:19:05,760
I'll let you know if anyone's interested.

503
00:19:05,940 --> 00:19:08,480
Right? So, but that would be all that would be required.

504
00:19:09,840 --> 00:19:10,100
All right.

505
00:19:10,560 --> 00:19:13,020
Now, anyway, so asymmetric information sometimes has

506
00:19:13,020 --> 00:19:14,840
efficiency implications, which we've seen.

507
00:19:15,600 --> 00:19:19,700
But even when the market outcomes are inefficient,

508
00:19:20,060 --> 00:19:21,740
government may be unable to improve matters.

509
00:19:21,740 --> 00:19:22,860
So take moral hazard.

510
00:19:23,860 --> 00:19:24,160
All right.

511
00:19:24,160 --> 00:19:26,200
So what can government really do about moral hazard?

512
00:19:27,700 --> 00:19:28,660
There's not very much.

513
00:19:28,660 --> 00:19:30,360
If there's going to be any time there's insurance,

514
00:19:30,360 --> 00:19:31,900
there's automatically going to be moral hazard.

515
00:19:32,280 --> 00:19:36,080
If the government is, so if industry is saying

516
00:19:36,080 --> 00:19:38,840
that if you get sick, we take care of the cost, you don't,

517
00:19:39,260 --> 00:19:42,100
then what law could government possibly pass

518
00:19:42,100 --> 00:19:43,660
that would preserve your incentive

519
00:19:43,660 --> 00:19:44,500
to take care of yourself?

520
00:19:45,500 --> 00:19:47,520
Right? I mentioned if you really start thinking hard,

521
00:19:47,520 --> 00:19:48,480
you can come up with things like how

522
00:19:48,480 --> 00:19:51,020
about punishing insurance fraud?

523
00:19:51,960 --> 00:19:53,960
That's a pretty severe kind of asymmetric information

524
00:19:53,960 --> 00:19:56,240
if people just go and deliberately shoot themselves

525
00:19:56,240 --> 00:19:58,940
in the foot and then go and demand assistance.

526
00:19:58,940 --> 00:20:11,920
Things like that.


1
00:20:00,000 --> 00:20:05,080
