right? So, how well does that stuff replicate? Right? There's the lowest standard replication

203
00:36:46,400 --> 00:36:50,920
where if you use the very same data set and the very same methods, you get exactly the

204
00:36:50,920 --> 00:37:00,480
same results. So, that's one. For very good stuff, I found that tends to be pretty true.

205
00:37:00,480 --> 00:37:06,080
But if the standard is if we get a new data set and use new methods, say better methods,

206
00:37:06,080 --> 00:37:10,480
if we get better data and better methods, do we get the same results? That's one where

207
00:37:10,480 --> 00:37:20,160
it's much less clear. All right. So, and then, you know, economists also are often fond of

208
00:37:20,160 --> 00:37:24,720
using a more complex technique that does not actually seem better than the other technique,

209
00:37:24,720 --> 00:37:28,520
but it's just harder to do. And require, you know, so you have to be smarter to do it.

210
00:37:28,520 --> 00:37:33,080
So, my view is there are a lot of papers where the best research is actually just ordinarily

211
00:37:33,080 --> 00:37:39,440
squared, ordinarily squares, but it's too easy to do and the results are too obvious.

212
00:37:39,440 --> 00:37:42,560
And then people go and come up with weird instrumental variables estimators, which are

213
00:37:42,560 --> 00:37:47,760
harder to do and there's more room for rationality. But as to why people believe those results

214
00:37:47,760 --> 00:37:51,880
more, I have no idea. It seems like, well, you assume that thing's exogenous. Why would

215
00:37:51,880 --> 00:37:55,960
anyone think that's exogenous? Well, I mean, it's like we can get like a bunch of papers

216
00:37:55,960 --> 00:38:01,440
out of it. So, I guess that's the reason. All right. Okay. So, just for background,

217
00:38:01,440 --> 00:38:05,240
right. So, behavioral approach and choice theory. It is rare for economists to think

218
00:38:05,240 --> 00:38:10,000
about empirically testing fundamental micro choice theory. So, you know the stuff that

219
00:38:10,000 --> 00:38:14,040
we do in the first few weeks of economics? Very unusual for someone to say, let's go

220
00:38:14,040 --> 00:38:19,560
and empirically test that stuff. All right. Why? Well, first of all, the very elementary

221
00:38:19,560 --> 00:38:24,440
stuff seems almost true by definition. Like when you say that preferences are transitive.

222
00:38:24,440 --> 00:38:30,120
It's like, so if you prefer A to B and B to C, then you prefer A to C. It's like, doesn't

223
00:38:30,120 --> 00:38:33,880
that kind of have to be true? Sort of seems really obvious. Like why would you bother

224
00:38:33,880 --> 00:38:41,320
testing something like that? All right. Now, if we assume that preferences are stable,

225
00:38:41,320 --> 00:38:46,400
which is what we almost always do in empirical work, then there's a lot of testable implications.

226
00:38:46,400 --> 00:38:51,000
So think about this. Suppose that you want to go and test the law of demand. All right.

227
00:38:51,000 --> 00:38:56,000
So, you say, all right, well, here's the law of demand. Right here's the demand curve.

228
00:38:56,000 --> 00:38:59,640
And then we go and we find out the price started off here and then it moved to here. Then you

229
00:38:59,640 --> 00:39:03,960
might want to see whether the quantity that people can sit and respond. All right. So

230
00:39:03,960 --> 00:39:07,880
that sounds like a good approach, but there's just one problem. If preferences are not stable

231
00:39:07,880 --> 00:39:12,520
and you appear to see that, you could also say something else that's totally consistent

232
00:39:12,520 --> 00:39:17,840
with basic theory. What can you say if, you know, so in other words, suppose that, let's

233
00:39:17,840 --> 00:39:24,200
see, the price goes up and instead the quantity demanded does that. All right. So first observation

234
00:39:24,200 --> 00:39:28,480
here, low price. Second observation here, high price. What can you always say about

235
00:39:28,480 --> 00:39:30,480
a demand curve if you don't believe in stable preferences?

236
00:39:30,480 --> 00:39:33,640
Well, if you think it's not stable, then the demand curve should be.

237
00:39:33,640 --> 00:39:37,400
Yeah. So you can say, no, no, no, no. Theory is still totally true. That. All right. So

238
00:39:37,400 --> 00:39:43,240
now I fit both data points. Okay. So theory is great. That's actually not empirical testing

239
00:39:43,240 --> 00:39:47,080
at all. Right. That's just going and drawing a line through every point and saying the

240
00:39:47,080 --> 00:39:53,400
theory is sound. Right. So that's CSO. In practice, then economists normally will assume

241
00:39:53,400 --> 00:39:57,840
stable preferences when they do empirical work. Because otherwise it's too easy. Otherwise

242
00:39:57,840 --> 00:40:00,120
you just go and see what happens and then say, well, clear.


1
00:40:00,000 --> 00:40:02,400
what occurred is there's a shift in demand and a curve.

2
00:40:03,140 --> 00:40:06,280
Okay, so usually we economists don't like to just go

3
00:40:06,280 --> 00:40:08,200
and assume that preferences randomly change.

4
00:40:08,720 --> 00:40:11,840
Now if you're talking about supply there, they can go

5
00:40:11,840 --> 00:40:14,180
and look at things like input prices and say, ah, well,

6
00:40:14,520 --> 00:40:16,520
we'll only like we could say the supply chains

7
00:40:16,520 --> 00:40:18,380
of input prices change if it's demand.

8
00:40:18,820 --> 00:40:21,640
Then if you've got some very clear predictors of demand

9
00:40:21,640 --> 00:40:24,780
like demand for skiing in the winter,

10
00:40:25,180 --> 00:40:26,500
then you might say, okay, that's fine.

11
00:40:26,500 --> 00:40:27,740
We'll go and we'll put in shell

12
00:40:27,740 --> 00:40:30,700
like these identifiable weather shifters into the model

13
00:40:30,960 --> 00:40:32,200
and then we can go and test it.

14
00:40:32,760 --> 00:40:35,420
But the idea that someone might just wake up and say,

15
00:40:35,420 --> 00:40:36,860
I just don't like skiing anymore, man,

16
00:40:37,100 --> 00:40:38,420
or that a whole country could wake up.

17
00:40:38,500 --> 00:40:39,560
We don't like skiing anymore.

18
00:40:40,080 --> 00:40:42,620
That idea is one that economists normally resist

19
00:40:42,620 --> 00:40:45,380
and empirical work in economics usually doesn't say stuff

20
00:40:45,380 --> 00:40:45,800
like that.

21
00:40:46,740 --> 00:40:47,040
All right.

22
00:40:48,440 --> 00:40:50,900
So once you put in stable preferences,

23
00:40:50,900 --> 00:40:54,000
then we can definitely go and empirically test, basic theory.

24
00:40:54,760 --> 00:40:57,320
And then another thing, another assumption,

25
00:40:57,320 --> 00:40:59,840
so besides the assumption of stable preferences,

26
00:41:01,280 --> 00:41:03,600
if we go further and assume

27
00:41:03,600 --> 00:41:05,220
that people have selfish preferences,

28
00:41:06,340 --> 00:41:08,980
then we can go even further towards testing basic micro.

29
00:41:09,940 --> 00:41:13,240
So if we assume that people are selfish, then we can go

30
00:41:13,240 --> 00:41:19,780
and test theories like what will landlords do if you go

31
00:41:19,780 --> 00:41:22,060
and impose rent control, right?

32
00:41:22,060 --> 00:41:24,840
Because suppose that landlords were totally unselfish

33
00:41:24,840 --> 00:41:25,760
and just wanted to help people.

34
00:41:25,760 --> 00:41:29,160
So all they do is they wake up and say, I want to go

35
00:41:29,160 --> 00:41:31,760
and provide housing for people who need houses.

36
00:41:32,260 --> 00:41:34,500
If that were the way that landlords thought and then you go

37
00:41:34,500 --> 00:41:37,420
and impose rent control, there's no reason for them

38
00:41:37,420 --> 00:41:39,000
to withdraw units from the market.

39
00:41:39,920 --> 00:41:43,940
So normally economists are assuming selfishness, right?

40
00:41:43,940 --> 00:41:47,800
And if you think about that Adam Smith quote

41
00:41:47,800 --> 00:41:49,200
that economists love to invoke,

42
00:41:49,740 --> 00:41:52,860
not from benevolence does the baker bake his bread, but from?

43
00:41:52,860 --> 00:41:56,120
Yes, his own regard to his own self-interest.

