of declaring Pluto no longer a planet.

206
00:47:43,940 --> 00:47:45,780
Why would astronomers do that?

207
00:47:46,260 --> 00:47:48,220
It's one of the worst possible things they could do

208
00:47:48,220 --> 00:47:51,500
for their field to go and take away a beloved frozen ice planet

209
00:47:51,500 --> 00:47:52,380
away for children.

210
00:47:52,820 --> 00:47:54,700
But they did it because they're like, I don't know,

211
00:47:54,700 --> 00:47:57,180
they have no sense of marketing at all I guess.

212
00:47:57,700 --> 00:47:57,940
All right.

213
00:47:59,220 --> 00:48:01,740
Now, the product of behavioral economics is a long list

214
00:48:01,740 --> 00:48:03,060
of what are called anomalies.

215
00:48:03,060 --> 00:48:04,300
Anomalies.

216
00:48:05,340 --> 00:48:05,620
All right.

217
00:48:06,820 --> 00:48:09,300
Now, a quick version of anomalies is just weird stuff

218
00:48:09,300 --> 00:48:15,620
we found, but the technical idea is that this is robust evidence

219
00:48:16,100 --> 00:48:18,940
that people sometimes are violating basic axioms

220
00:48:18,940 --> 00:48:19,500
of choice theory.

221
00:48:20,740 --> 00:48:23,260
Robust evidence that people are sometimes violating your basic

222
00:48:23,260 --> 00:48:24,220
axioms of choice theory.

223
00:48:24,740 --> 00:48:25,620
All right.

224
00:48:26,140 --> 00:48:29,260
And any of you, I'm pretty sure the column is long gone,

225
00:48:29,260 --> 00:48:31,060
but Richard Thaler used to have a column in the Journal

226
00:48:31,060 --> 00:48:34,340
of Economic Perspectives called Anomalies,

227
00:48:34,740 --> 00:48:37,460
where each article would be something that we see

228
00:48:37,460 --> 00:48:39,740
in the real world that seems to be predicted

229
00:48:39,980 --> 00:48:41,420
to not exist by economic theory.

230
00:48:43,420 --> 00:48:43,700
All right.

231
00:48:43,980 --> 00:48:48,260
Now, these violations of choice theory are also often called

232
00:48:48,260 --> 00:48:48,900
irrationality.

233
00:48:50,460 --> 00:48:51,780
So, irrationality.

234
00:48:52,620 --> 00:48:58,220
Right. And especially if you are an old economist.

235
00:48:58,220 --> 00:49:01,260
An economist, though, so old that you got your PhD before,

236
00:49:01,260 --> 00:49:02,260
say, 1973.

237
00:49:02,940 --> 00:49:03,060
All right.

238
00:49:03,060 --> 00:49:04,540
There's not that many left anymore,

239
00:49:04,540 --> 00:49:05,300
but they're still around.

240
00:49:05,740 --> 00:49:10,220
So, if you got your PhD before then, then you are likely to go

241
00:49:10,220 --> 00:49:12,700
and just treat these things as being exactly the same.

242
00:49:13,620 --> 00:49:14,020
All right.

243
00:49:14,740 --> 00:49:17,980
So, and what's the key is if you got your PhD

244
00:49:17,980 --> 00:49:19,860
before the Rational Expeditions Revolution,

245
00:49:20,220 --> 00:49:22,300
then you just think of rationality as being things

246
00:49:22,300 --> 00:49:23,580
like having a transitive behavior.

247
00:49:23,580 --> 00:49:27,460
Or it could also be things like counting sunk costs

248
00:49:27,460 --> 00:49:28,420
when you make your decisions.

249
00:49:29,100 --> 00:49:30,820
That would be another classic example.

250
00:49:31,500 --> 00:49:31,700
All right.

251
00:49:31,700 --> 00:49:33,740
So, how many people here have reached the point

252
00:49:33,740 --> 00:49:35,420
where you walk out of movies appropriately?

253
00:49:36,420 --> 00:49:38,900
Yes. How many are not quite there yet?

254
00:49:39,700 --> 00:49:42,300
Yes. Right now, of course, there's one thing if you're going

255
00:49:42,300 --> 00:49:43,540
to see movies with non-economists,

256
00:49:43,540 --> 00:49:44,580
and they'll just get mad at you.

257
00:49:44,580 --> 00:49:45,860
Then there's a reason to sit through it.

258
00:49:46,260 --> 00:49:48,420
But if you're all by, how many of you,

259
00:49:48,420 --> 00:49:50,220
so this is actually not technically predicted

260
00:49:50,220 --> 00:49:52,460
by economics, but I feel like economists ought to do it.

261
00:49:52,460 --> 00:49:54,020
I feel like economists ought to do this.

262
00:49:54,340 --> 00:49:55,540
See movies alone.

263
00:49:56,140 --> 00:49:57,540
Yes, it's not that social.

264
00:49:57,540 --> 00:49:58,860
Like, why don't you just go by yourself

265
00:49:58,860 --> 00:50:00,420
and see the movie you want when you want to see it?

266
00:50:00,780 --> 00:50:01,900
And when you're with other people,

267
00:50:01,900 --> 00:50:03,180
do something with the other people.

268
00:50:03,900 --> 00:50:04,140
All right.

269
00:50:04,140 --> 00:50:06,300
So, you know, or maybe you want to talk about the movie you

270
00:50:06,300 --> 00:50:07,260
for or after or whatever.

271
00:50:07,260 --> 00:50:10,780
But still, there I think there is a stigma

272
00:50:10,940 --> 00:50:14,340
that economists must Vulcan mind meld to overcome.

273
00:50:14,900 --> 00:50:15,180
All right.

274
00:50:16,060 --> 00:50:18,620
So, Tyler talked, here I will thank Tyler.

275
00:50:18,620 --> 00:50:20,060
Tyler got me to start seeing movies alone,

276
00:50:20,060 --> 00:50:21,020
start walking out of movies.

277
00:50:21,020 --> 00:50:22,100
I'm eternally grateful.

278
00:50:22,100 --> 00:50:22,420
It's great.

279
00:50:22,980 --> 00:50:23,260
Okay.

280
00:50:23,580 --> 00:50:26,140
It's Tyler Cowan made you antisocial.

281
00:50:26,460 --> 00:50:30,580
Well, made me fit for a good society.

282
00:50:30,580 --> 00:50:35,540
A society we don't have yet, but one day we'll because of what he

283
00:50:35,540 --> 00:50:36,820
said to me that one day.

284
00:50:36,820 --> 00:50:38,780
But everything is going to fall into place.

285
00:50:40,540 --> 00:50:42,300
Now, we're going to go over some

286
00:50:42,300 --> 00:50:43,580
of the main documented anomalies.

287
00:50:43,580 --> 00:50:46,260
But first, I'm just going to pre-answer some

288
00:50:46,260 --> 00:50:47,540
of the blanket objections.

289
00:50:47,540 --> 00:50:51,380
So, these are arguments that economists often make to say,

290
00:50:51,380 --> 00:50:53,540
look, why do I not even have to read this garbage?

291
00:50:54,420 --> 00:50:56,740
Why do I not need to even open up the textbook

292
00:50:56,740 --> 00:50:57,740
to see what's inside?

293
00:50:57,820 --> 00:51:01,540
Why can I just pre-dismiss it all so I don't even have

294
00:51:01,540 --> 00:51:03,900
to judge it case by case on whether the evidence is any good?

295
00:51:03,900 --> 00:51:07,460
I can just say that it's ruled out on methodological grounds.

296
00:51:08,380 --> 00:51:08,740
All right.

297
00:51:09,660 --> 00:51:10,580
So, what are they?

298
00:51:11,180 --> 00:51:15,700
First one, this is where the economist plays philosopher

299
00:51:15,700 --> 00:51:16,220
of science.

300
00:51:16,220 --> 00:51:18,180
So, you start hearing about anomalies.

301
00:51:18,180 --> 00:51:20,740
You say, well, look, all theories are false.

302
00:51:21,780 --> 00:51:23,100
What matters is prediction.

303
00:51:24,100 --> 00:51:26,980
So, where does this all theories are false stuff come from?

304
00:51:26,980 --> 00:51:31,820
Yeah, Friedman and, you know, so, you know, so,

305
00:51:31,820 --> 00:51:33,580
Popper is kind of in there.

306
00:51:33,580 --> 00:51:35,900
I actually think Popper is to blame too.

307
00:51:35,900 --> 00:51:37,820
Even, you know, somebody said, well, that's kind of weird.

308
00:51:37,820 --> 00:51:39,340
He's the father of falsificationism.

309
00:51:39,340 --> 00:51:42,300
So, the idea is that, like, every theory is going

310
00:51:42,300 --> 00:51:43,900
to have problems, but, and we just keep,

311
00:51:43,900 --> 00:51:45,740
we have a theory that's going to have problems.

312
00:51:45,740 --> 00:51:48,100
We have a theory until you show me the problem,

313
00:51:48,100 --> 00:51:48,900
that's the best we can do.

314
00:51:48,900 --> 00:51:50,420
Everything's going to fail eventually.

315
00:51:50,860 --> 00:51:52,980
But if you haven't found a problem yet,

316
00:51:52,980 --> 00:51:54,420
that's the best that we can expect.

317
00:51:55,100 --> 00:51:55,340
All right.

318
00:51:56,140 --> 00:51:56,900
All theories are false.

319
00:51:56,900 --> 00:51:57,940
What matters is prediction.

320
00:51:58,460 --> 00:52:01,500
So, in one reply is, there's plenty of,

321
00:52:01,500 --> 00:52:03,900
plenty of the anomalies evidence is showing the predictions

322
00:52:03,900 --> 00:52:04,220
are wrong.

323
00:52:05,580 --> 00:52:09,100
So, in that case, the objection is just really not,

324
00:52:09,100 --> 00:52:10,900
is just really not relevant.

325
00:52:10,900 --> 00:52:12,900
And on top of it, this all theories

326
00:52:12,900 --> 00:52:15,620
are false idea is strange in itself.

327
00:52:15,620 --> 00:52:18,140
You might say, well, all theories are false

328
00:52:18,140 --> 00:52:20,780
to if you demand on accuracy to an infinite number

329
00:52:20,780 --> 00:52:21,740
of decimal places.

330
00:52:23,140 --> 00:52:26,140
Fine. But what if your theory is false

331
00:52:26,140 --> 00:52:27,460
to the first decimal place?

332
00:52:28,660 --> 00:52:31,060
What if, like, the first digit is completely wrong?

333
00:52:32,260 --> 00:52:34,500
Right? You know, so, and, you know, of course,

334
00:52:34,780 --> 00:52:37,420
very often people actually, when they're thinking

335
00:52:37,420 --> 00:52:38,660
about whether a theory is relevant,

336
00:52:38,660 --> 00:52:39,500
you have to say, well,

337
00:52:39,780 --> 00:52:42,900
does the real world even closely match the assumptions?

338
00:52:43,340 --> 00:52:45,540
So, for example, suppose that someone wanted to show

339
00:52:45,540 --> 00:52:47,140
that perfect competition was wrong.

340
00:52:47,500 --> 00:52:49,380
And the way you do it is you go and test the model

341
00:52:49,380 --> 00:52:53,700
on say government supply of water.

342
00:52:53,980 --> 00:52:54,740
When government supply, you know,

343
00:52:54,740 --> 00:52:56,860
like a monopoly water company.

344
00:52:56,860 --> 00:52:58,780
And then you say, see, perfect competition's wrong.

345
00:53:00,260 --> 00:53:02,500
Right? What's the obvious thing to say in defense

346
00:53:02,500 --> 00:53:04,380
of the perfectly competitive model if you test it

347
00:53:04,740 --> 00:53:07,420
on the monopoly water company?

348
00:53:09,140 --> 00:53:12,820
Yes. It's not a theory about what monopolies do.

349
00:53:12,820 --> 00:53:15,660
It's a theory about what competitors do.

350
00:53:15,700 --> 00:53:18,100
So, no matter what you're saying,

351
00:53:18,100 --> 00:53:20,660
actually there's always some effort to see, well,

352
00:53:20,900 --> 00:53:23,940
do the basic assumptions of the model seem more or less right?

353
00:53:24,700 --> 00:53:27,260
Milton Friedman actually has this line where he says, look,

354
00:53:27,660 --> 00:53:31,500
the reason why Newtonian physics works isn't

355
00:53:31,500 --> 00:53:33,380
that the world is approximately a vacuum.

356
00:53:34,340 --> 00:53:35,060
No one's reading it.

357
00:53:35,500 --> 00:53:36,380
Yes, it is.

358
00:53:36,940 --> 00:53:37,940
Of course that's why.

359
00:53:38,260 --> 00:53:42,260
You know, if we're doing Newtonian physics in oil,

360
00:53:42,260 --> 00:53:45,140
then things don't fall according to that equation.

361
00:53:45,900 --> 00:53:49,900
So, why is it that objects fall very close,

362
00:53:49,900 --> 00:53:52,740
almost as Newton says they will through air?

363
00:53:53,300 --> 00:53:55,780
Because air, there's hardly any air actually.

364
00:53:55,780 --> 00:53:57,100
We're almost in a vacuum.

365
00:53:58,820 --> 00:53:59,940
Right? You know, like one atmosphere

366
00:53:59,940 --> 00:54:01,980
of pressure is almost no pressure actually.

367
00:54:03,060 --> 00:54:05,420
So, this is not a class in philosophy science.

368
00:54:05,420 --> 00:54:06,500
I'm not going to test you on this.

369
00:54:06,540 --> 00:54:10,580
But, yeah, worth pointing out that the fact

370
00:54:10,580 --> 00:54:12,420
that Friedman said it, you know, like how much did Friedman really

371
00:54:12,420 --> 00:54:13,500
know about philosophy of science?

372
00:54:13,500 --> 00:54:16,540
It seems like he just sort of read a few things and said,

373
00:54:16,540 --> 00:54:18,860
well, now all economists have to go

374
00:54:18,860 --> 00:54:20,580
with this one controversial philosophy,

375
00:54:20,900 --> 00:54:22,500
which apparently is the only one I read about.

376
00:54:23,340 --> 00:54:24,700
You know, you can be a super smart guy,

377
00:54:24,700 --> 00:54:28,180
but if you only read one out of a dozen different views,

378
00:54:28,700 --> 00:54:30,820
the fact that you adopted is really not that convincing.

379
00:54:32,300 --> 00:54:32,660
All right.

380
00:54:33,220 --> 00:54:35,900
Now, second objection to the anomaly say, look,

381
00:54:35,900 --> 00:54:38,580
sure there's all kinds of random error, but it all cancels out.

382
00:54:38,580 --> 00:54:41,660
And the standard answer is no, they don't cancel out.

383
00:54:42,100 --> 00:54:43,980
The errors are actually systematic.

384
00:54:44,420 --> 00:54:45,460
There's a pattern of error,

385
00:54:46,020 --> 00:54:48,460
such as people don't walk out of movies.

386
00:54:49,380 --> 00:54:51,540
Right? It's not like sometimes people are really enjoying a

387
00:54:51,540 --> 00:54:53,140
movie and they randomly walk out.

388
00:54:53,460 --> 00:54:55,140
And other times they're really hating a movie

389
00:54:55,140 --> 00:54:56,340
and they randomly stay in.

390
00:54:56,700 --> 00:54:58,420
Instead, there seems to be a general pattern

391
00:54:58,420 --> 00:55:01,180
that people are not enjoying a movie, sit in there watching it

392
00:55:01,660 --> 00:55:03,220
because they already paid their money.

393
00:55:04,660 --> 00:55:04,940
All right.

394
00:55:04,940 --> 00:55:07,580
And, of course, if that argument no longer makes any sense

395
00:55:07,580 --> 00:55:08,820
to you, congratulations.

396
00:55:08,900 --> 00:55:11,180
So you are ready for your PhD in economics.

397
00:55:11,620 --> 00:55:14,460
But if it still is of appeal to you, then you still have

398
00:55:14,460 --> 00:55:18,780
to burn out that last remnant of humanity and just say, no.

399
00:55:19,780 --> 00:55:22,340
It is ridiculous to sit there suffering

400
00:55:22,340 --> 00:55:24,820
through a movie you don't like just because you already paid.

401
00:55:25,540 --> 00:55:26,340
Yeah, Ash?

402
00:55:26,540 --> 00:55:28,340
Yeah, to me what makes it even less sense than sitting

403
00:55:28,340 --> 00:55:30,020
through a whole movie is reading a whole book.

404
00:55:30,820 --> 00:55:33,940
Right? A movie is over in a couple hours.

405
00:55:33,940 --> 00:55:35,020
Yeah, yeah, sure.

406
00:55:35,020 --> 00:55:42,060
I think a lot more people don't finish books.

407
00:55:42,940 --> 00:55:45,340
Although also books are much less likely to be starved.

408
00:55:45,340 --> 00:55:46,900
I don't know.

409
00:55:47,620 --> 00:55:48,020
All right.

410
00:55:48,460 --> 00:55:49,700
And then a final objection.

411
00:55:49,780 --> 00:55:52,900
This one says anomalies arise purely due to weak incentives.

412
00:55:53,620 --> 00:55:54,580
And, indeed, it's true

413
00:55:54,580 --> 00:55:56,180
that in most psychology experiments there are no

414
00:55:56,180 --> 00:55:57,260
monetary incentives.

415
00:55:57,820 --> 00:56:00,820
And in economics experiments,

416
00:56:01,020 --> 00:56:02,740
often the incentives are very weak.

417
00:56:02,740 --> 00:56:05,740
So oftentimes there are experiments where all

418
00:56:05,740 --> 00:56:07,380
that happens is that if you do well,

419
00:56:07,380 --> 00:56:08,580
then your name is entered in the lottery.

420
00:56:08,580 --> 00:56:10,780
So only one person in the entire room even gets paid.

421
00:56:10,780 --> 00:56:13,260
So you might say, oh, well, people are acting this way

422
00:56:13,260 --> 00:56:15,060
because of weak incentives.

423
00:56:15,660 --> 00:56:18,500
So now a reply that is often made, this is one

424
00:56:18,500 --> 00:56:19,860
that I think actually is overstated,

425
00:56:19,860 --> 00:56:21,180
but at least it's a common reply,

426
00:56:21,620 --> 00:56:23,620
is that strong incentives often don't matter.

427
00:56:24,620 --> 00:56:25,900
Furthermore, many people said a lot

428
00:56:25,900 --> 00:56:27,620
of the big anomalies appear in financial markets

429
00:56:27,620 --> 00:56:29,540
where it seems like there's actually huge incentives

430
00:56:29,980 --> 00:56:30,620
to get it right.

431
00:56:30,620 --> 00:56:32,020
And yet people keep doing it wrong.

432
00:56:32,020 --> 00:56:36,100
Right now a point that I like to push is even

433
00:56:36,100 --> 00:56:38,420
if this is only true, these results are still really

434
00:56:38,420 --> 00:56:41,820
important because actually we make a lot of choices

435
00:56:41,820 --> 00:56:43,820
that have very weak incentives.

436
00:56:44,660 --> 00:56:46,340
Most obviously for voting.

437
00:56:47,900 --> 00:56:49,660
Voting is a choice that we make

438
00:56:49,660 --> 00:56:51,220
that has very big social implications,

439
00:56:51,220 --> 00:56:52,540
but there's very little incentive

440
00:56:52,540 --> 00:56:53,940
to vote one way versus the other.

441
00:56:54,340 --> 00:56:57,300
So even if you thought that weak incentives changed behavior,

442
00:56:57,700 --> 00:56:59,580
there are a number of kinds of behavior

443
00:56:59,580 --> 00:57:00,540
where the incentives are weak.

444
00:57:00,540 --> 00:57:02,900
So in that case the behavioral economics should give us a

445
00:57:02,900 --> 00:57:05,380
better prediction of behavior than the regular stuff.

446
00:57:05,740 --> 00:57:08,260
Or use another one I like, how about charitable donation?

447
00:57:08,900 --> 00:57:11,220
What selfish incentive do you have to go

448
00:57:11,220 --> 00:57:13,100
and carefully research charities to make sure

449
00:57:13,100 --> 00:57:15,180
that the world is getting the most bang for your buck?

450
00:57:17,180 --> 00:57:17,940
Not much.

451
00:57:18,620 --> 00:57:23,100
Right? So if weak incentives lead to bad behavior,

452
00:57:23,420 --> 00:57:26,380
then what might you think that philanthropists will do

453
00:57:26,380 --> 00:57:28,020
when they go in and hand out their money?

454
00:57:28,020 --> 00:57:30,620
Or fail to do is a better way.

455
00:57:30,620 --> 00:57:34,340
What would philanthropists generally fail to do?

456
00:57:35,900 --> 00:57:39,020
Research. And there's actually a whole movement dedicated

457
00:57:39,020 --> 00:57:42,540
to trying to improve this behavior called effective?

458
00:57:42,900 --> 00:57:43,300
Alterism.

459
00:57:43,300 --> 00:57:44,260
Effective alterism.

460
00:57:44,780 --> 00:57:46,300
Right? Where they look at the world and they say, look,

461
00:57:46,300 --> 00:57:48,300
it seems like there's a lot of charitable donation,

462
00:57:48,580 --> 00:57:51,100
the dollars are massive, but the accomplishment is small

463
00:57:51,100 --> 00:57:54,060
because people spend so much money on tons of stupid stuff.

464
00:57:54,060 --> 00:57:56,740
So at least it's an interesting thing to consider.

465
00:57:56,740 --> 00:57:59,340
Maybe people don't really research it in the same way

466
00:57:59,340 --> 00:58:01,020
that they would research their portfolio

467
00:58:01,380 --> 00:58:03,580
because the main reason people give

468
00:58:03,580 --> 00:58:05,900
to charity perhaps is not actually to accomplish good,

469
00:58:05,900 --> 00:58:06,980
but to feel like they're doing good.

470
00:58:06,980 --> 00:58:08,500
And you can get the feeling of doing good

