01:00:34,240 --> 01:00:36,640
But, I'm not sure.

10
01:00:36,640 --> 01:00:39,960
Alright, so questions before we get going.

11
01:00:39,960 --> 01:00:46,160
Alright, let's start on one of the earliest kinds of results that economists started doing.

12
01:00:46,160 --> 01:00:50,320
Actually, this is one psychologist started and then economists went and did their own

13
01:00:50,320 --> 01:00:53,160
studies adding on incentives to see what would make a difference.

14
01:00:53,160 --> 01:00:58,760
Alright, so as I said, for practical purposes, economists almost always assume that human

15
01:00:58,760 --> 01:01:04,640
beings have constant preferences, stable preferences over outcomes.

16
01:01:04,640 --> 01:01:09,680
But behavioral economists have found a bunch of what appear to be counter examples.

17
01:01:09,680 --> 01:01:16,680
So, a single individual will prefer A to B or B to A for reasons that seem irrelevant.

18
01:01:16,680 --> 01:01:19,400
And when this happens, it's called a preference reversal.

19
01:01:19,400 --> 01:01:21,320
What are some examples?

20
01:01:21,320 --> 01:01:27,280
Similarly equivalent descriptions of the same situation will lead to different choices.

21
01:01:27,280 --> 01:01:31,400
So, there is an experiment where they actually had medical students.

22
01:01:31,400 --> 01:01:35,640
Now this is a hypothetical example, but nevertheless it's experimental because they're randomly

23
01:01:35,640 --> 01:01:38,760
assigned to get one or the other version of the question.

24
01:01:38,760 --> 01:01:43,000
So some of the medical students are told, suppose there's a treatment that has a 90%

25
01:01:43,000 --> 01:01:45,440
chance of working.

26
01:01:45,440 --> 01:01:46,520
Should you do that?

27
01:01:46,520 --> 01:01:49,520
And then the other one, suppose you have a treatment that has a 10% chance of failing.

28
01:01:49,520 --> 01:01:50,520
Should you do that?

29
01:01:50,520 --> 01:01:55,800
And in both cases, success and failure are defined in the same way.

30
01:01:55,800 --> 01:02:01,520
So it's just a matter of saying 90% success or 10% failure.

31
01:02:01,520 --> 01:02:04,520
And this, by definition, seems to be the same thing.

32
01:02:04,520 --> 01:02:08,520
90% success equals 10% failure.

33
01:02:08,520 --> 01:02:14,720
And yet actually, the rates at which medical students say that they would use a treatment

34
01:02:14,720 --> 01:02:18,240
depends upon whether you describe it as success or failure.

35
01:02:18,240 --> 01:02:22,160
This is something you can also pretty clearly see in all of marketing.

36
01:02:22,160 --> 01:02:25,840
Like in marketing, do you ever want to go and talk about how our product doesn't suck

37
01:02:25,840 --> 01:02:29,280
as much as the other products that might be competing for your attention?

38
01:02:29,280 --> 01:02:31,520
No, you want to say it's even better.

39
01:02:31,520 --> 01:02:34,260
It's the very best of these.

40
01:02:34,260 --> 01:02:38,280
Now logically, again, to say, look, it's the least bad of all of them.

41
01:02:38,280 --> 01:02:39,440
Same thing.

42
01:02:39,440 --> 01:02:42,240
But you don't want to say it that way.

43
01:02:42,240 --> 01:02:43,760
Anyone will advise you.

44
01:02:43,760 --> 01:02:49,440
Just like if you are proposing marriage, you want to say, look, I'm the best fit for you

45
01:02:49,440 --> 01:02:50,440
and you're the best fit for me.

46
01:02:50,440 --> 01:02:54,520
You don't want to say, we are the least bad fits for each other out of the choices that

47
01:02:54,520 --> 01:02:58,920
are available in this world, which is the same thing.

48
01:02:58,920 --> 01:03:02,360
But it's not a good way to say it.

49
01:03:02,360 --> 01:03:06,480
Right now, some people instinctively understand this kind of thing.

50
01:03:06,480 --> 01:03:10,960
Like my younger son is a natural advertising executive.

51
01:03:10,960 --> 01:03:16,280
So he's always looking for a way to put whatever he wants in the most favorable possible light.

52
01:03:16,280 --> 01:03:20,520
So it's like, thank you, mommy, for being a nice mommy that would let me use the tablet

53
01:03:20,520 --> 01:03:21,520
all that I want.

54
01:03:21,520 --> 01:03:22,520
All right.

55
01:03:22,520 --> 01:03:24,800
It's like, hmm.

56
01:03:24,800 --> 01:03:29,840
So there appears to be a combination of a bunch of different claims that you're blending

57
01:03:29,840 --> 01:03:32,240
in there, trying to package as a compliment.

58
01:03:32,240 --> 01:03:37,280
But there's also a thinly veiled threat of you are not a nice mommy if you don't give

59
01:03:37,280 --> 01:03:38,800
me this tablet to play with.

60
01:03:38,800 --> 01:03:39,800
All right.

61
01:03:39,800 --> 01:03:42,680
So that's the way that a lot of marketing works.

62
01:03:42,680 --> 01:03:47,400
And when you guys are looking for jobs, I strongly urge you to apply the wisdom of the

63
01:03:47,400 --> 01:03:48,400
ages.

64
01:03:48,400 --> 01:03:54,800
You don't want to go and tell people, look, I don't think you can do better than me.

65
01:03:54,800 --> 01:03:57,560
That's equivalent to I am your best option.

66
01:03:57,560 --> 01:04:05,920
But the first way of saying it sounds insulting and pessimistic and emphasizing just the wretchedness

67
01:04:05,920 --> 01:04:06,920
of man.

68
01:04:06,920 --> 01:04:09,640
You don't want to do that when you're selling yourself.

69
01:04:09,640 --> 01:04:15,000
You want to say, look, I understand a school of your caliber has many attractive options.

70
01:04:15,000 --> 01:04:20,000
But I think that our fit between us goes beyond that.

71
01:04:20,000 --> 01:04:22,760
We are meant for each other.

72
01:04:22,760 --> 01:04:25,200
I am meant to do my work with you, and you are meant to have me.

73
01:04:25,200 --> 01:04:27,520
Of course, you should do it better than that.

74
01:04:27,520 --> 01:04:32,880
So there's always a question of, will it blow up in your face if you lay the flattery on

75
01:04:32,880 --> 01:04:35,320
too thick?

76
01:04:35,320 --> 01:04:38,480
I've actually occasionally said this.

77
01:04:38,480 --> 01:04:42,680
So I remember once back in 1999, there was a guy interviewing here.

