94
01:05:23,560 --> 01:05:24,560
OK.

95
01:05:24,560 --> 01:05:27,600
Now, another example of preference reversal.

96
01:05:27,600 --> 01:05:31,320
So people sometimes select the choice that they put less monetary value on.

97
01:05:31,320 --> 01:05:35,320
So remember, the way that economists normally think about the way we order things is that

98
01:05:35,320 --> 01:05:39,120
you'll always choose the thing that you put the highest dollar value on.

99
01:05:39,120 --> 01:05:42,200
So if you have five options, what's your willingness to pay for each one?

100
01:05:42,200 --> 01:05:44,560
And then, well, which one are you going to pick?

101
01:05:44,560 --> 01:05:47,160
It's going to be the one you put the highest dollar value on.

102
01:05:47,160 --> 01:05:52,000
And yet, in experiments, people sometimes don't do this.

103
01:05:52,000 --> 01:05:57,600
So there's a classic experiment where you give people the choice of two bets.

104
01:05:57,600 --> 01:06:04,680
And you say, look, you can either have an 8 ninth chance of $4, or that's one possibility,

105
01:06:04,680 --> 01:06:10,000
or you can have a 1 ninth chance of $40.

106
01:06:10,000 --> 01:06:12,680
And this one is called high.

107
01:06:12,680 --> 01:06:14,200
This one is called low.

108
01:06:14,200 --> 01:06:16,240
All right.

109
01:06:16,240 --> 01:06:21,240
So normally, people prefer this one if they have a choice, if they're choosing them.

110
01:06:21,240 --> 01:06:26,680
But if you give the person a gamble and try to buy it back for them, they want more money

111
01:06:26,680 --> 01:06:30,040
for that one, even though this is the one that they would pick.

112
01:06:30,040 --> 01:06:32,040
And that's kind of weird.

113
01:06:32,040 --> 01:06:36,160
They may say, yeah, those choices are really similar to each other.

114
01:06:36,160 --> 01:06:37,160
Let's see.

115
01:06:37,160 --> 01:06:39,800
So here we have an expected value of 32 ninths.

116
01:06:39,800 --> 01:06:41,080
There we have 40 ninths.

117
01:06:41,080 --> 01:06:43,440
They're very close to each other.

118
01:06:43,440 --> 01:06:49,760
But nevertheless, it is odd that people would generally like this one more, but want more

119
01:06:49,760 --> 01:06:51,720
money to sell that one.

120
01:06:51,720 --> 01:06:53,400
That's right.

121
01:06:53,400 --> 01:06:54,400
OK.

122
01:06:54,400 --> 01:06:56,160
All right.

123
01:06:56,160 --> 01:06:58,840
So these preference reversals have got a lot of attention.

124
01:06:58,840 --> 01:07:03,320
I think there's at least 100 articles published by economists on preference reversals.

125
01:07:03,320 --> 01:07:05,200
And psychologists started it.

126
01:07:05,200 --> 01:07:10,560
So the economists who did the earliest experiments, at least their story is they thought that

127
01:07:10,560 --> 01:07:14,600
they could disprove the psychologists, but then lo and behold, the psychologists are

128
01:07:14,600 --> 01:07:15,600
right.

129
01:07:15,600 --> 01:07:18,120
I'm a little suspicious of that theory, of that story, actually.

130
01:07:18,120 --> 01:07:23,040
But in any case, lots of research on this by economists, and then before that, plenty

131
01:07:23,040 --> 01:07:24,640
in psychology.

132
01:07:24,640 --> 01:07:27,520
So how important is it in the real world?

133
01:07:27,520 --> 01:07:30,200
I'm not convinced it's that big of a deal.

134
01:07:30,200 --> 01:07:31,200
All right.

135
01:07:31,200 --> 01:07:32,200
So yeah.

136
01:07:32,200 --> 01:07:35,000
So it is a good idea to try to say the glass is half full if you want to persuade people

137
01:07:35,000 --> 01:07:37,200
rather than saying the glass is half empty.

138
01:07:37,200 --> 01:07:38,480
So that's common sense.

139
01:07:38,480 --> 01:07:40,120
That's true.

140
01:07:40,120 --> 01:07:45,480
But normally, the experiments get choices that are really close to each other and then

141
01:07:45,480 --> 01:07:47,760
get it, and then get these reversals.

142
01:07:47,760 --> 01:07:50,880
They don't get choices that are really different from each other.

