All right, so selfishness and cooperation.

44
01:42:39,760 --> 01:42:44,120
Peer theorists very rarely mention selfishness, or in fact, I've heard many peer theorists

45
01:42:44,120 --> 01:42:50,640
actually deny flatly that economics has any assumption of selfishness baked into it.

46
01:42:50,640 --> 01:42:55,720
But almost all real world applications of economic theory do assume narrowly selfish

47
01:42:55,720 --> 01:42:56,720
behavior.

48
01:42:56,720 --> 01:42:58,800
This is actually what's going on.

49
01:42:58,800 --> 01:43:00,640
This is why we caught Adam Smith.

50
01:43:00,640 --> 01:43:05,360
When we say socialism just doesn't work, whenever we say like incentives matter, tragedy of

51
01:43:05,360 --> 01:43:10,320
the commons, whenever we talk about rent control, whenever we talk about taxes, underlying all

52
01:43:10,320 --> 01:43:14,800
of this is the idea that people don't like working for strangers for free.

53
01:43:14,800 --> 01:43:17,840
People don't like just giving stuff to strangers.

54
01:43:17,840 --> 01:43:21,560
People don't want to suffer just because it benefits society.

55
01:43:21,560 --> 01:43:24,600
This is, there are a few people like that maybe.

56
01:43:24,600 --> 01:43:27,400
There's a lot of people who claim to be like that who aren't.

57
01:43:27,400 --> 01:43:31,440
And there's a whole lot of people who just grump and are, no, I don't want to.

58
01:43:31,440 --> 01:43:33,040
All right.

59
01:43:33,040 --> 01:43:37,240
So we have almost all real world applications assume that people are narrowly selfish.

60
01:43:37,240 --> 01:43:42,880
Now a slight variation on this, which a number of economists have picked up from the field

61
01:43:42,880 --> 01:43:53,480
of evolutionary psychology, is something called inclusive fitness.

62
01:43:53,480 --> 01:43:56,120
Right now, how many of you have ever heard of a book called The Selfish Gene by Richard

63
01:43:56,120 --> 01:43:57,120
Dawkins?

64
01:43:57,120 --> 01:43:58,120
Yes.

65
01:43:58,120 --> 01:44:01,440
So that is probably still the best popularization of the idea of inclusive fitness.

66
01:44:01,440 --> 01:44:10,240
So the idea here is that human beings, though we love being alive and doing all of our stuff,

67
01:44:10,240 --> 01:44:11,800
but also we like all other animals.

68
01:44:11,800 --> 01:44:14,160
We are here because of our genes.

69
01:44:14,160 --> 01:44:19,560
And genes that don't propagate themselves tend to die out.

70
01:44:19,560 --> 01:44:23,280
We, the very fact that we're here indicates we got the kind of genes that haven't died

71
01:44:23,280 --> 01:44:24,280
out so far.

72
01:44:24,280 --> 01:44:25,280
We've made it so far.

73
01:44:25,280 --> 01:44:26,280
Right?

74
01:44:26,280 --> 01:44:31,600
And now when you start thinking about this, there are many ways this just simply, this

75
01:44:31,600 --> 01:44:32,600
predicts narrow selfishness.

76
01:44:32,600 --> 01:44:38,960
Like, so, I suppose there were cavemen a million years ago who went and gave all their food

77
01:44:38,960 --> 01:44:43,320
away to total strangers with no expectation of ever being paid back.

78
01:44:43,320 --> 01:44:45,800
What's likely to happen to cavemen like that?

79
01:44:45,800 --> 01:44:46,920
They die.

80
01:44:46,920 --> 01:44:49,600
So we are not their descendants.

81
01:44:49,600 --> 01:44:53,120
We're the descendants of the cavemen who did not like to go and throw and give their food

82
01:44:53,120 --> 01:44:54,920
away to total strangers for free.

83
01:44:54,920 --> 01:44:57,040
All right, pretty obvious point.

84
01:44:57,040 --> 01:45:01,200
Now, Inclusive Fitness goes and adds on extra insight that says, yeah, but suppose a million

85
01:45:01,200 --> 01:45:06,320
years ago there was a caveman who looked at his baby and said, you little beggar, what

86
01:45:06,320 --> 01:45:07,920
are you ever going to do for me?

87
01:45:07,920 --> 01:45:09,600
Well, you're like, you can't even talk.

88
01:45:09,600 --> 01:45:12,400
You're not, you won't even remember what I've ever done for you.

89
01:45:12,400 --> 01:45:15,200
And I could easily be dead by the time you're old enough to help me out.

90
01:45:15,200 --> 01:45:16,560
So forget you, little baby.

91
01:45:16,560 --> 01:45:22,320
All right, so imagine that there were some cavemen who treated their own biological offspring

92
01:45:22,320 --> 01:45:25,040
with that level of apathy.

93
01:45:25,040 --> 01:45:30,760
All right, well, what happened to those babies?

94
01:45:30,760 --> 01:45:31,760
They died off.

95
01:45:31,760 --> 01:45:33,880
So how many descendants did those cavemen have?

96
01:45:33,880 --> 01:45:34,880
None.

97
01:45:34,880 --> 01:45:38,160
So we are not descended from those cavemen either.

98
01:45:38,160 --> 01:45:42,080
We're descended from the cavemen who didn't like giving stuff to total strangers for free

99
01:45:42,080 --> 01:45:44,600
but love their own blood.

100
01:45:44,600 --> 01:45:46,760
Love their own blood relations.

101
01:45:46,760 --> 01:45:49,600
Now how much do they need to love their own blood relations?

102
01:45:49,600 --> 01:45:55,360
This is where inclusive fitness gets interesting because there's a whole set of fractions associated

103
01:45:55,360 --> 01:45:58,280
with the degree of blood relation.

104
01:45:58,280 --> 01:46:03,880
So for example, with a biological sibling or a biological child, what fraction of your

105
01:46:03,880 --> 01:46:05,440
genes do you approximately share?

106
01:46:05,440 --> 01:46:06,440
One.

107
01:46:06,440 --> 01:46:07,440
One half.

108
01:46:07,440 --> 01:46:08,440
One half.

109
01:46:08,440 --> 01:46:10,960
Yes, one half.

110
01:46:10,960 --> 01:46:15,320
On the other hand, with say a cousin, how many, so if you have a first cousin, what

111
01:46:15,320 --> 01:46:20,640
fraction of your genes do you have in common with your first cousin?

112
01:46:20,640 --> 01:46:21,640
An eighth.

113
01:46:21,640 --> 01:46:22,640
An eighth.

114
01:46:22,640 --> 01:46:31,480
Yes, because remember that, so, so with your own, so if with your brother, say, so I have

115
01:46:31,480 --> 01:46:36,360
half my brother's genes and his daughter has half of his genes, so my niece has one fourth

116
01:46:36,360 --> 01:46:40,800
of my genes, but my kids only have half of mine, so they actually only have an eighth

117
01:46:40,800 --> 01:46:41,800
each.

118
01:46:41,800 --> 01:46:43,240
All right.

119
01:46:43,240 --> 01:46:44,240
And so on.

120
01:46:44,240 --> 01:46:46,280
So you can go and crunch all this math.

121
01:46:46,280 --> 01:46:50,440
It's actually more complicated in the real world because there's actually mild incest

122
01:46:50,440 --> 01:46:52,560
going on all the time.

123
01:46:52,560 --> 01:46:56,780
So for example, if you marry within your own ethnicity, then you probably actually share

124
01:46:56,780 --> 01:47:02,080
more than half the genes with your kids because you are a distant relation of your spouse.

125
01:47:02,080 --> 01:47:03,080
All right.

126
01:47:03,080 --> 01:47:06,720
So I don't want to gross anybody out, but that's what's going on.

127
01:47:06,720 --> 01:47:07,720
All right.

128
01:47:07,720 --> 01:47:11,800
Now, this led, well, I think it was actually the founder of inclusive fitness theory, Hamilton,

129
01:47:11,800 --> 01:47:15,200
and he was asked, would you die for your brother, he said, no, but I would die for two brothers

130
01:47:15,200 --> 01:47:17,200
or eight cousins.

131
01:47:17,200 --> 01:47:19,480
All right.

132
01:47:19,480 --> 01:47:25,360
Now that sounds a little bit stronger than reality and support, but again, the idea of

133
01:47:25,360 --> 01:47:30,160
this is that when we are evaluating human selfishness, we should be looking at it not

134
01:47:30,160 --> 01:47:35,800
through the narrow view of the, of the interests of the individual, but rather through this,

135
01:47:35,800 --> 01:47:39,040
rather through the genes eye point of view, because that's really what we're evolved to

136
01:47:39,040 --> 01:47:40,780
be like.

137
01:47:40,780 --> 01:47:45,320
For most purposes, of course, it doesn't matter because normally we are trading with people

138
01:47:45,320 --> 01:47:47,440
that we're not related to.

139
01:47:47,440 --> 01:47:52,240
But what you're doing, say economics of the family, then suddenly it's very important

140
01:47:52,240 --> 01:47:56,480
because now suddenly we're dealing with people that we are, that we are very closely related

141
01:47:56,480 --> 01:47:57,480
to.

142
01:47:57,480 --> 01:48:00,480
They are an extension of oneself in a way.

143
01:48:00,480 --> 01:48:01,840
All right.

144
01:48:01,840 --> 01:48:08,360
So now, so if you've got perfectly selfish people who are not relatives at all, there

145
01:48:08,360 --> 01:48:10,800
are still funny ways that you can get cooperation there.

146
01:48:10,800 --> 01:48:12,440
We already talked about them when we did game theory.

147
01:48:12,440 --> 01:48:15,240
So you've got two people, they're not related at all.

148
01:48:15,240 --> 01:48:18,160
They don't intrinsically care about each other at all.

149
01:48:18,160 --> 01:48:20,520
How can they get, make cooperation work?

150
01:48:20,520 --> 01:48:24,200
Well, we talked about, so game, game, remember game theory.

151
01:48:24,200 --> 01:48:27,160
What's the obvious way they can make cooperation work?

152
01:48:27,160 --> 01:48:29,720
Starts with an R. Repeated play.

153
01:48:29,720 --> 01:48:31,080
It's repeated play.

154
01:48:31,080 --> 01:48:34,960
So that's why I mentioned going and giving all your food away to a stranger will never

155
01:48:34,960 --> 01:48:36,320
reciprocate.

156
01:48:36,320 --> 01:48:41,960
If you go and give some food to another person who you know, who lives around you, who might

157
01:48:41,960 --> 01:48:45,320
have food when you don't one day, and you can go and remind them, hey, remember when

158
01:48:45,320 --> 01:48:47,280
I bailed you out, now it's your turn.

159
01:48:47,280 --> 01:48:48,280
Right?

160
01:48:48,280 --> 01:48:51,800
If that kind of thing is going on, then it may be totally selfish to go and help out

161
01:48:51,800 --> 01:48:56,320
someone around you because you scratch my back and I scratch yours.

162
01:48:56,320 --> 01:49:00,720
And you know, general rule is the world is ruled by friendship.

163
01:49:00,720 --> 01:49:03,720
So you want to cultivate friendships as many as possible.

164
01:49:03,720 --> 01:49:08,160
This means on Facebook, you should not be trying to make enemies out of people.

165
01:49:08,160 --> 01:49:11,580
You don't know who might know someone who knows someone who can get you a job.

166
01:49:11,580 --> 01:49:13,480
So be nice to everybody.

167
01:49:13,480 --> 01:49:17,800
Make cultivate friends and try to be Mr. Nice, Mr. Nice person.

168
01:49:17,800 --> 01:49:21,560
So smart and interesting, but also completely lovable.

169
01:49:21,560 --> 01:49:22,560
Right?

170
01:49:22,560 --> 01:49:24,480
So you have someone that everyone wants to be around.

171
01:49:24,480 --> 01:49:28,080
That is a very good strategy to apply, right?

172
01:49:28,080 --> 01:49:31,120
Because you are hoping that they will remember, oh, this is a good person here that we can

173
01:49:31,120 --> 01:49:32,120
work with.

174
01:49:32,120 --> 01:49:37,120
And we have proven this in the past, so let's go and give back to this person who has given

175
01:49:37,120 --> 01:49:43,160
us years of entertaining Facebook posts without a hint of negativity.

176
01:49:43,160 --> 01:49:44,160
Okay.

177
01:49:44,160 --> 01:49:45,160
All right.

178
01:49:45,160 --> 01:49:49,520
So mere cooperation is not good evidence against human selfishness.

179
01:49:49,520 --> 01:49:52,520
The mere fact that people trade with each other doesn't show that we are not totally

180
01:49:52,520 --> 01:49:53,520
selfish.

181
01:49:53,520 --> 01:49:54,920
Maybe we are and it's just repeated play.

182
01:49:54,920 --> 01:49:59,400
Lately, I have been reading all of the academic research on trust.

183
01:49:59,400 --> 01:50:02,600
And again, the way that people talk, sometimes they make it sound like they do believe that

184
01:50:02,600 --> 01:50:08,680
there is no human reason to interact with other people, to cooperate, other than trust.

185
01:50:08,680 --> 01:50:12,600
Other than, well, we just have a feeling that other people, we just have a feeling of trust

186
01:50:12,600 --> 01:50:14,600
and that's the only possible way.

187
01:50:14,600 --> 01:50:16,880
Well, maybe it isn't trust.

188
01:50:16,880 --> 01:50:19,760
Maybe it's reciprocity that you just know that what comes around goes around.

189
01:50:19,760 --> 01:50:21,560
You don't need any trust for that to work.

190
01:50:21,560 --> 01:50:22,560
Right?

191
01:50:22,560 --> 01:50:26,680
Or I'm also very tempted to say, have you ever seen a credit card?

192
01:50:26,680 --> 01:50:31,960
I deal with people all the time that I have no actual fundamental trust in, but I trust

193
01:50:31,960 --> 01:50:33,920
the Visa Corporation.

194
01:50:33,920 --> 01:50:35,920
The Visa Corporation, them I believe in.

195
01:50:35,920 --> 01:50:44,120
Visa, the flag of Visa flies high and I say, yes, Visa.

196
01:50:44,120 --> 01:50:48,080
I believe in them way more than any of these different people that I trade with on Amazon

197
01:50:48,080 --> 01:50:49,080
Marketplace.

198
01:50:49,080 --> 01:50:52,560
Because look, if they're okay, according to the Visa Corporation, that's good enough for

199
01:50:52,560 --> 01:50:53,560
me.

200
01:50:53,560 --> 01:50:55,480
But otherwise, would I trust them?

201
01:50:55,480 --> 01:50:56,480
I don't think I trust them at all.

202
01:50:56,480 --> 01:50:57,480
Who are you?

203
01:50:57,480 --> 01:50:58,480
Whatever.

204
01:50:58,480 --> 01:50:59,480
Okay.

205
01:50:59,480 --> 01:51:00,480
All right.

206
01:51:00,480 --> 01:51:07,200
Now, how then can we actually establish that people are not totally selfish all the time?

207
01:51:07,200 --> 01:51:11,080
So step one is we can look at the experimental evidence, which we've already talked about.

208
01:51:11,080 --> 01:51:16,880
There's plenty of cooperation even in one shot games where there's no opportunity for

209
01:51:16,880 --> 01:51:21,160
repeat, where there's total anonymity and yet we see cooperation.

210
01:51:21,160 --> 01:51:25,280
So that would be some pretty compelling evidence that people are not selfish all the time.

211
01:51:25,280 --> 01:51:26,280
Right?

212
01:51:26,280 --> 01:51:28,560
So why would they be doing it?

213
01:51:28,560 --> 01:51:30,360
You don't have any idea who I am.

214
01:51:30,360 --> 01:51:32,160
We don't repeat the game.

215
01:51:32,160 --> 01:51:36,600
What possible motive could you have other than I am not a completely selfish person?

216
01:51:36,600 --> 01:51:40,080
It's hard to say what the other reason would be.

217
01:51:40,080 --> 01:51:41,280
All right.

218
01:51:41,280 --> 01:51:45,760
In the original Superman 2, the original and good General Zod, not the monstrosity from

219
01:51:45,760 --> 01:51:50,200
the last one, but the original one played by Terrence Stamp, there is a great scene

220
01:51:50,200 --> 01:51:57,200
in this movie when the three villains from Krypton are fighting Superman and they accidentally

221
01:51:57,200 --> 01:52:02,280
go and drop a building on a bunch of innocent people and Superman rushes to go and save

222
01:52:02,280 --> 01:52:03,280
them.

223
01:52:03,280 --> 01:52:08,520
And then General Zod says, ha ha, I've discovered Superman's weakness.

224
01:52:08,520 --> 01:52:11,480
And then I think his girlfriend says, oh, what is it?

225
01:52:11,480 --> 01:52:15,040
He says, he actually cares about these earthlings.

226
01:52:15,040 --> 01:52:20,400
And then she's like, as pets?

227
01:52:20,400 --> 01:52:21,400
Presumably.

228
01:52:21,400 --> 01:52:22,400
So that was his reaction.

229
01:52:22,400 --> 01:52:26,360
Complete puzzlement that someone would care about the earthlings.

230
01:52:26,360 --> 01:52:29,880
But that actually seems to be often the case.

231
01:52:29,880 --> 01:52:30,880
All right.

232
01:52:30,880 --> 01:52:34,840
So as I said, just remember we did these, we talked about public goods experiments.

233
01:52:34,840 --> 01:52:40,560
In one shot games, it's very normal to contribute 40 to 60% of the socially optimal balance.

234
01:52:40,560 --> 01:52:43,440
Which again, you might just say is because people don't understand the game.

235
01:52:43,440 --> 01:52:47,760
But even after they've been playing for a while, you can generally get results up to

236
01:52:47,760 --> 01:52:52,080
or cooperation up to pretty high levels again just by saying, OK, time out.

237
01:52:52,080 --> 01:52:54,040
All right, new round, people.

238
01:52:54,040 --> 01:52:55,040
Let's start the game over.

239
01:52:55,040 --> 01:52:59,440
And then again, people generally start cooperating at these moderate levels again.

240
01:52:59,440 --> 01:53:00,440
All right.

241
01:53:00,440 --> 01:53:04,240
As we said, cooperation does tend to decline with repeated play.

242
01:53:04,240 --> 01:53:08,040
And remember, this is repeated play where it's a big group, so there's no real hope

243
01:53:08,040 --> 01:53:09,480
of punishing anybody.

244
01:53:09,480 --> 01:53:11,680
It's just like we're going on and on.

245
01:53:11,680 --> 01:53:14,760
And the people that cooperate keep losing money and getting more and more depressed

246
01:53:14,760 --> 01:53:16,880
about the nature of man and so on.

247
01:53:16,880 --> 01:53:18,280
And then cooperation goes down.

248
01:53:18,280 --> 01:53:22,040
Like I said, if you restart a tournament with experienced players, we get this initial jump

249
01:53:22,040 --> 01:53:23,040
up in cooperation again.

250
01:53:23,040 --> 01:53:26,940
And then we got a lot of extra experimental elements too.

251
01:53:26,940 --> 01:53:27,940
So tipping.

252
01:53:27,940 --> 01:53:32,680
In general, you are not legally required to tip anything.

253
01:53:32,680 --> 01:53:35,920
So and if you remember, if you ever seen the beginning of the movie Reservoir Dogs, there's

254
01:53:35,920 --> 01:53:38,640
a guy who tells a bunch of gangsters, I don't tip.

255
01:53:38,640 --> 01:53:39,640
And they're all horrified.

256
01:53:39,640 --> 01:53:40,640
What do you mean you don't tip?

257
01:53:40,640 --> 01:53:42,040
That's terrible.

258
01:53:42,040 --> 01:53:46,320
We murder people and rob banks, but we tip, like all decent people.

259
01:53:46,320 --> 01:53:47,320
All right.

260
01:53:47,320 --> 01:53:48,320
So tipping.

261
01:53:48,320 --> 01:53:52,160
You could just take 15% off the bill of what you're paying or whatever you tip and just

262
01:53:52,160 --> 01:53:53,160
not do it.

263
01:53:53,160 --> 01:53:56,320
Again, of course, if you repeatedly go to the same restaurant, I wouldn't recommend

264
01:53:56,320 --> 01:53:57,320
this.

265
01:53:57,320 --> 01:54:01,280
Then they might start spitting in your food or giving you really slow service or saying

266
01:54:01,280 --> 01:54:03,520
we don't even want your business here.

267
01:54:03,520 --> 01:54:07,160
But very often, people go to a restaurant they're never going to go to again.

268
01:54:07,160 --> 01:54:09,340
No one's ever going to remember you.

269
01:54:09,340 --> 01:54:13,440
You can walk out before they even go and look at how much you tipped.

270
01:54:13,440 --> 01:54:16,040
And nevertheless, tipping rates remain extremely high.

271
01:54:16,040 --> 01:54:21,840
So and then you say, well, that's just because people are going in groups.

272
01:54:21,840 --> 01:54:26,440
Nobody tips when they're alone and they're in a place that they don't normally go.

273
01:54:26,440 --> 01:54:27,440
Really?

274
01:54:27,440 --> 01:54:28,440
I do.

275
01:54:28,440 --> 01:54:33,280
At least I say I do.

276
01:54:33,280 --> 01:54:34,280
So let's see.

277
01:54:34,280 --> 01:54:37,000
Anyone here ever work as a server in a restaurant?

278
01:54:37,000 --> 01:54:44,240
So how often was it that if a person came in all by themselves and they were not a regular

279
01:54:44,240 --> 01:54:46,360
customer, what fraction of them just tip zero?

280
01:54:46,360 --> 01:54:48,360
I don't think that's ever happened.

281
01:54:48,360 --> 01:54:49,360
Yes.

282
01:54:49,360 --> 01:54:51,960
So it's still extraordinarily rare to just tip zero.

283
01:54:51,960 --> 01:54:57,720
I mean, so again, among people who work, there's all kinds of informal knowledge about who

284
01:54:57,720 --> 01:55:00,000
tips how much and why.

285
01:55:00,000 --> 01:55:03,000
So like I've heard the rule that the elderly barely tip at all.

286
01:55:03,000 --> 01:55:07,080
And furthermore, I've heard the rule that you can never tell whether they're satisfied

287
01:55:07,080 --> 01:55:08,080
