429
01:75:55,160 --> 01:75:56,560
it's not really altruism either.

430
01:75:56,560 --> 01:75:59,600
It seems to be motivated by some kind of fairness norm.

431
01:75:59,600 --> 01:76:02,160
And the fairness norm used to be that you tip 10%.

432
01:76:02,160 --> 01:76:05,120
When I was a kid, it was 10%, and it was really easy to do it.

433
01:76:05,120 --> 01:76:08,640
Then you get 15%, not so, not so, a little bit harder.

434
01:76:08,640 --> 01:76:11,720
18%, very confusing.

435
01:76:11,720 --> 01:76:14,600
So some people now make it 20%.

436
01:76:14,600 --> 01:76:17,520
And of course, now notice one other thing.

437
01:76:17,520 --> 01:76:19,140
If you're traveling in another country,

438
01:76:19,140 --> 01:76:22,960
people commonly ask, what do people tip around here,

439
01:76:22,960 --> 01:76:24,360
or do they?

440
01:76:24,360 --> 01:76:26,760
And whatever it is that is normal in that country,

441
01:76:26,760 --> 01:76:30,000
people generally acclimate to pretty easily.

442
01:76:30,000 --> 01:76:33,800
Because it's not that you really care about servers,

443
01:76:33,800 --> 01:76:36,240
or that you say, well, in Japan, I don't care about them,

444
01:76:36,240 --> 01:76:37,360
but in America, I do.

445
01:76:37,360 --> 01:76:40,320
Rather, say, well, in Japan, tipping is not customary.

446
01:76:40,320 --> 01:76:43,280
So it's not unfair to fail to tip.

447
01:76:43,280 --> 01:76:45,560
In fact, I've heard that if you try to tip in Japan,

448
01:76:45,560 --> 01:76:46,360
it's insulting.

449
01:76:46,360 --> 01:76:47,440
Is that correct?

450
01:76:47,440 --> 01:76:47,940
Yes?

451
01:76:47,940 --> 01:76:51,240
I used to live in Japan, and yeah, it's condescending.

452
01:76:51,240 --> 01:76:51,920
Yes.

453
01:76:51,920 --> 01:76:53,960
Will they still take the money, or will they pass it back

454
01:76:53,960 --> 01:76:54,800
to you and be angry?

455
01:76:54,800 --> 01:76:55,400
I never try.

456
01:76:55,400 --> 01:76:55,900
Oh.

457
01:76:55,900 --> 01:76:58,880
So you ever see an American go and try tipping, and then?

458
01:76:58,880 --> 01:77:01,080
So everybody now, sorry.

459
01:77:01,080 --> 01:77:04,440
So when I'm in Europe, so the first day or two

460
01:77:04,440 --> 01:77:05,740
I'm in a European country, that's

461
01:77:05,740 --> 01:77:07,320
where it's very hard to adjust.

462
01:77:07,320 --> 01:77:11,040
It's like, I'm just going to pay the bill, and then walk away.

463
01:77:11,040 --> 01:77:11,840
It's kind of weird.

464
01:77:11,840 --> 01:77:13,520
And then, especially when they say, well,

465
01:77:13,520 --> 01:77:16,440
it's customary to sort of give whatever pocket change you have.

466
01:77:16,440 --> 01:77:18,000
Well, I don't have any pocket change.

467
01:77:18,000 --> 01:77:19,000
What do I do?

468
01:77:19,000 --> 01:77:20,400
I don't want people here to think

469
01:77:20,400 --> 01:77:22,640
that I'm a big American jerk.

470
01:77:22,640 --> 01:77:25,400
But then, after a few days, then you

471
01:77:25,400 --> 01:77:27,320
start to feel, OK, this is what's normal here.

472
01:77:27,320 --> 01:77:30,200
OK, now I think I understand what people looks at.

473
01:77:30,200 --> 01:77:31,620
Well, every now and then, there's

474
01:77:31,620 --> 01:77:32,920
someone who'll say, well, he's an American,

475
01:77:32,920 --> 01:77:34,800
so he's supposed to do the American thing.

476
01:77:34,800 --> 01:77:36,520
No, I thought we were in Germany,

477
01:77:36,520 --> 01:77:37,680
and we do the German thing.

478
01:77:37,680 --> 01:77:38,360
No, no, no.

479
01:77:38,360 --> 01:77:41,240
Americans have to be American even here.

480
01:77:41,240 --> 01:77:42,720
No.

481
01:77:42,720 --> 01:77:43,440
All right.

482
01:77:43,440 --> 01:77:44,920
So anyway, that's fairness.

483
01:77:44,920 --> 01:77:50,680
Now, another motive that we see experimentally, as well as

484
01:77:50,680 --> 01:77:52,200
seem to experience introspectively,

485
01:77:52,200 --> 01:77:55,120
is called vindictiveness.

486
01:77:55,120 --> 01:77:56,240
Vindictiveness.

487
01:77:56,240 --> 01:77:59,360
All right, so this is what we can see in the ultimatum game.

488
01:77:59,360 --> 01:78:01,320
If you offer less than an even split,

489
01:78:01,320 --> 01:78:04,640
there's a very good chance that the other person will cut off

490
01:78:04,640 --> 01:78:10,120
his nose to spite his face and say, $4 out of 10,

491
01:78:10,120 --> 01:78:11,280
I don't think so.

492
01:78:11,280 --> 01:78:13,200
How about no dollars for either of us?

493
01:78:13,200 --> 01:78:17,040
Ha, you're stuck on that, greedy jerk.

494
01:78:17,040 --> 01:78:19,200
All right, that is a pretty common reaction

495
01:78:19,200 --> 01:78:22,680
to uneven splits.

496
01:78:22,680 --> 01:78:24,200
All right, now you may say, well,

497
01:78:24,200 --> 01:78:25,040
but what do you care?

498
01:78:25,040 --> 01:78:26,080
He's a complete stranger.

499
01:78:26,080 --> 01:78:29,400
It's just a game, and you just gave up $4 to hurt someone

500
01:78:29,400 --> 01:78:31,000
that you don't even know?

501
01:78:31,000 --> 01:78:33,400
That's right.

502
01:78:33,400 --> 01:78:37,800
A lot of people have this reaction to the experiment.

503
01:78:37,800 --> 01:78:41,080
So the idea is that, especially when we're treated unfairly,

504
01:78:41,080 --> 01:78:42,920
we think we've been treated unfairly,

505
01:78:42,920 --> 01:78:46,960
then we actually are worse than economists dangerously assume.

506
01:78:46,960 --> 01:78:49,720
We then put negative weight on other people.

507
01:78:49,720 --> 01:78:51,200
So normally, of course, economists

508
01:78:51,200 --> 01:78:52,360
just say people are selfish.

509
01:78:52,360 --> 01:78:54,440
You don't put any positive weight on other people.

510
01:78:54,440 --> 01:78:55,480
But there's experimental evidence

511
01:78:55,480 --> 01:78:57,720
that we sometimes put negative weight on other people.

512
01:78:57,720 --> 01:79:00,440
Another way in which it would be really good if people were just

513
01:79:00,440 --> 01:79:02,840
regular old homo economicus, with regular old homo

514
01:79:02,840 --> 01:79:05,160
economicus, no one ever wants to go and wipe out

515
01:79:05,160 --> 01:79:07,840
all the members of another group because what one member did.

516
01:79:07,840 --> 01:79:10,560
No one wants to go and kill off other people.

517
01:79:10,560 --> 01:79:14,520
It's just like, hey, well, what's done is done.

518
01:79:14,520 --> 01:79:15,560
What can I do about it?

519
01:79:15,560 --> 01:79:18,520
You might want to go and punish the actual person who did it

520
01:79:18,520 --> 01:79:21,360
because you want a reputation, but to go and just

521
01:79:21,360 --> 01:79:24,920
punish a random person because you're like, well,

522
01:79:24,920 --> 01:79:26,160
a redhead went and hurt me.

523
01:79:26,160 --> 01:79:29,000
Let's go and hurt another redhead.

524
01:79:29,000 --> 01:79:32,280
That would never occur to homo economicus,

525
01:79:32,280 --> 01:79:35,000
but to an vindictive person, it might.

526
01:79:35,000 --> 01:79:37,520
It's a really weird idea.

527
01:79:37,520 --> 01:79:41,200
People say stuff like this frequently.

528
01:79:41,200 --> 01:79:43,040
So if you know anyone who's ever made

529
01:79:43,040 --> 01:79:44,600
a point of just getting in a fight

530
01:79:44,600 --> 01:79:46,360
over some small and petty offense,

531
01:79:46,360 --> 01:79:49,040
either a physical fight or maybe just start yelling at someone

532
01:79:49,040 --> 01:79:52,200
for no particular reason.

533
01:79:52,200 --> 01:79:53,960
If you just sort of look around and you

534
01:79:53,960 --> 01:79:55,840
see people yelling at others and getting angry,

535
01:79:55,840 --> 01:79:58,300
or I can just look at my dad and see him getting mad at people

536
01:79:58,300 --> 01:80:06,040
and getting in a fight.


1
01:80:00,000 --> 01:80:01,760
getting into petty disputes with people

2
01:80:01,760 --> 01:80:03,700
over very small amounts of money.

3
01:80:05,640 --> 01:80:06,980
He's not even really retired,

4
01:80:06,980 --> 01:80:08,760
so he has a decent opportunity cost,

5
01:80:08,760 --> 01:80:11,560
but nevertheless, he will go out of his way

6
01:80:11,560 --> 01:80:15,480
to go and say, I'm very angry about this here,

7
01:80:15,480 --> 01:80:18,080
and I wanna talk to the manager.

8
01:80:18,080 --> 01:80:20,080
Like, that's gonna do any good.

9
01:80:20,080 --> 01:80:22,360
But that's the way that my dad is,

10
01:80:22,360 --> 01:80:24,760
and he's not the only one for sure.

11
01:80:24,760 --> 01:80:27,060
So plenty of people get angry at others,

12
01:80:27,060 --> 01:80:28,800
and then wanna get back at them.

13
01:80:28,800 --> 01:80:30,360
I'm gonna get back at them.

14
01:80:30,360 --> 01:80:32,040
All right, so you may think about

15
01:80:32,040 --> 01:80:34,440
whether you've ever wanted to get back at somebody.

16
01:80:34,440 --> 01:80:35,280
All right.

17
01:80:37,320 --> 01:80:38,360
So that's the idea.

18
01:80:38,360 --> 01:80:40,080
All right, so real-world significance

19
01:80:40,080 --> 01:80:43,520
of fairness and vindictiveness.

20
01:80:43,520 --> 01:80:44,920
So pretty easy to observe.

21
01:80:44,920 --> 01:80:47,200
So we wouldn't have tipping without fairness norms probably.

22
01:80:47,200 --> 01:80:49,120
At least tipping would be very different.

23
01:80:49,120 --> 01:80:50,520
There'd only be tipping in places

24
01:80:50,520 --> 01:80:52,720
where there's repeated customers,

25
01:80:52,720 --> 01:80:54,800
or of course, where you go in groups.

26
01:80:55,760 --> 01:80:58,280
Now, the funny thing about the whole,

27
01:80:58,280 --> 01:80:59,840
the idea that people tip as much as they do

28
01:80:59,840 --> 01:81:01,280
because other people are watching,

29
01:81:01,280 --> 01:81:04,040
is actually restaurants seem to have the opposite theory.

30
01:81:04,040 --> 01:81:05,840
Right, because it's very common for restaurants to say

31
01:81:05,840 --> 01:81:07,920
that if the party has, say, six or more people,

32
01:81:07,920 --> 01:81:09,560
what happens automatically?

33
01:81:09,560 --> 01:81:10,760
The crudity's put in.

34
01:81:10,760 --> 01:81:12,460
The crudity's put in.

35
01:81:12,460 --> 01:81:13,920
So there it seems like contrary to theory,

36
01:81:13,920 --> 01:81:15,280
that people only tip because they wanna go

37
01:81:15,280 --> 01:81:17,500
and impress their friends with how generous they are.

38
01:81:17,500 --> 01:81:18,840
Actually, when there's a large group,

39
01:81:18,840 --> 01:81:20,600
the tragedy of the commons kicks in,

40
01:81:20,600 --> 01:81:22,480
and everybody starts skimping,

41
01:81:22,480 --> 01:81:25,000
because it will be the whole group

42
01:81:25,000 --> 01:81:26,520
that will be regarded as an unfair group

43
01:81:26,520 --> 01:81:28,080
rather than any one individual in the group

44
01:81:28,080 --> 01:81:29,280
will be seen as unfair.

45
01:81:30,360 --> 01:81:33,040
All right, so even when there's no repeated interaction,

46
01:81:33,040 --> 01:81:34,520
we give up small personal benefits

47
01:81:34,520 --> 01:81:35,760
to do what fairness requires,

48
01:81:35,760 --> 01:81:37,720
expect others to do the same.

49
01:81:37,720 --> 01:81:40,760
We might think that prices and wages would be more flexible

50
01:81:40,760 --> 01:81:43,880
if we didn't have these fairness and vindictiveness norms,

51
01:81:43,880 --> 01:81:45,160
because a lot of people have it in their heads

52
01:81:45,160 --> 01:81:47,680
that it's unfair for prices to change,

53
01:81:47,680 --> 01:81:49,880
and if a price unfairly changes,

54
01:81:49,880 --> 01:81:52,120
it's okay to say shoplift,

55
01:81:52,120 --> 01:81:57,120
or go and, say, buy something and then return it

56
01:81:57,120 --> 01:81:59,440
and get your money back after you mess it up,

57
01:81:59,440 --> 01:82:03,040
or something that was noted with a lot of foreclosures.

58
01:82:03,040 --> 01:82:05,560
Very often, when a home is foreclosed upon,

59
01:82:05,560 --> 01:82:08,360
what do the occupants do before they leave?

60
01:82:09,440 --> 01:82:13,920
Trash the place, rip out the toilets.

61
01:82:13,920 --> 01:82:15,600
It's one thing to go and try and recover

62
01:82:15,600 --> 01:82:16,960
valuable items from the house,

63
01:82:16,960 --> 01:82:18,560
like rip out the copper wire,

64
01:82:18,560 --> 01:82:20,720
but just to go and put holes in the walls

65
01:82:20,720 --> 01:82:24,080
to get back at the bank for taking your house away?

66
01:82:24,080 --> 01:82:25,040
All right, this, in the end,

67
01:82:25,040 --> 01:82:27,640
seemed like it was a pretty big deal,

68
01:82:27,640 --> 01:82:31,360
and of course, it winds up having some bad effects,

69
01:82:31,360 --> 01:82:32,760
because in general equilibrium,

70
01:82:32,760 --> 01:82:35,160
the banks know that if they ever foreclose their home,

71
01:82:35,160 --> 01:82:36,520
that you will trash it.

72
01:82:36,520 --> 01:82:38,360
This does what to the interest rates

73
01:82:38,360 --> 01:82:40,120
that everybody has to pay?

74
01:82:40,120 --> 01:82:42,000
That increases, and especially, of course,

75
01:82:42,000 --> 01:82:43,200
if you are a high-risk borrower,

76
01:82:43,200 --> 01:82:45,680
high-risk borrowers have to pay especially high rates

77
01:82:45,680 --> 01:82:47,360
because they're especially likely to foreclose,

78
01:82:47,360 --> 01:82:49,120
which means that the bank's especially likely

79
01:82:49,120 --> 01:82:51,220
to not get back its full investment.

80
01:82:51,220 --> 01:82:54,460
All right, so things like this.

81
01:82:54,460 --> 01:82:58,460
Now, one example that at least used to be talked about a lot

82
01:82:58,460 --> 01:83:00,020
was shortages of concert tickets

83
01:83:00,020 --> 01:83:03,220
when concert tickets would be priced well below markets,

84
01:83:03,220 --> 01:83:06,580
especially for very exciting or big or important events.

85
01:83:06,580 --> 01:83:08,180
I've heard people talk about this a lot less

86
01:83:08,180 --> 01:83:09,980
than they used to.

87
01:83:09,980 --> 01:83:11,340
Has it actually become less common

88
01:83:11,340 --> 01:83:13,980
for tickets to be underpriced?

89
01:83:13,980 --> 01:83:14,820
Or have you noticed?

90
01:83:14,820 --> 01:83:16,900
So how common is it that, say,

91
01:83:16,900 --> 01:83:19,140
tickets for popular concerts sell out

92
01:83:19,140 --> 01:83:21,660
within a few minutes and then there's no way

93
01:83:21,660 --> 01:83:22,860
of getting tickets?

94
01:83:22,860 --> 01:83:23,820
It's still common.

95
01:83:23,820 --> 01:83:26,700
It's still common, so maybe it's possibly less common

96
01:83:26,700 --> 01:83:28,100
than it used to be, but again,

97
01:83:28,100 --> 01:83:29,100
this is something where I can't tell

98
01:83:29,100 --> 01:83:30,140
whether I've just gotten older

99
01:83:30,140 --> 01:83:32,380
and so I don't talk to people who complain about it

100
01:83:32,380 --> 01:83:34,380
or whether the world has actually changed.

101
01:83:34,380 --> 01:83:36,120
It's certainly much easier to resell stuff

102
01:83:36,120 --> 01:83:37,220
than it used to be.

103
01:83:37,220 --> 01:83:38,060
Yeah.

104
01:83:38,060 --> 01:83:40,060
So it may be that the actual harm of the norms

105
01:83:40,060 --> 01:83:42,340
is much less because now they get bought up quickly

106
01:83:42,340 --> 01:83:43,580
and then they get resold quickly,

107
01:83:43,580 --> 01:83:46,260
and then in the end, it's still going to be rich people

108
01:83:46,260 --> 01:83:48,940
who are the ones who attend the concerts.

109
01:83:48,940 --> 01:83:50,620
So that might be what's going on.

110
01:83:50,620 --> 01:83:51,940
Isn't there an embarrassment factor there

111
01:83:51,940 --> 01:83:53,540
if you don't sell out?

112
01:83:53,540 --> 01:83:54,380
I mean, you wanna-

113
01:83:54,380 --> 01:83:55,940
Yeah, so that is a theory people are told,

114
01:83:55,940 --> 01:84:00,100
but normally people say there's sort of like a pride factor

115
01:84:00,100 --> 01:84:02,620
in selling out, but couldn't there also be a pride factor

116
01:84:02,620 --> 01:84:06,320
in charging a normal ticket price successfully?

117
01:84:07,340 --> 01:84:09,120
So similarly, it seems like there could be a shame factor

118
01:84:09,120 --> 01:84:11,340
in making your tickets really cheap, right?

119
01:84:11,340 --> 01:84:13,740
So like if the tickets are a dollar, wow, we sold out,

120
01:84:13,740 --> 01:84:15,300
but that's pretty embarrassing.

121
01:84:16,140 --> 01:84:18,700
So I mean, there is a theory that you get some advertising

122
01:84:18,700 --> 01:84:22,700
out of the news saying tickets sold out instantly, maybe,

123
01:84:22,700 --> 01:84:25,500
but like, what's it feel like if they always sell out,

124
01:84:25,500 --> 01:84:27,700
then why do you want that advertising?

125
01:84:27,700 --> 01:84:30,140
It's not so clear what the point of that is.

126
01:84:30,140 --> 01:84:30,980
All right.

127
01:84:32,340 --> 01:84:35,500
So when we do labor economics, we'll talk more about

128
01:84:35,500 --> 01:84:37,940
the role of fairness norms in labor markets.

129
01:84:37,940 --> 01:84:40,180
You know, over time, I've become more and more convinced

130
01:84:40,180 --> 01:84:43,740
that actually labor markets are heavily affected

131
01:84:43,740 --> 01:84:46,820
by these norms and very much for the worse.

132
01:84:46,820 --> 01:84:49,740
It would be far better if people would just have

133
01:84:49,740 --> 01:84:52,020
a more Vulcan attitude about it and realize

134
01:84:52,020 --> 01:84:53,940
it's only business, it's not personal,

135
01:84:53,940 --> 01:84:56,260
there's no reason to get offended about the way

136
01:84:56,260 --> 01:84:58,620
that people pay, and there's certainly no reason

137
01:84:58,620 --> 01:85:00,780
to try to get revenge on someone.

138
01:85:00,780 --> 01:85:03,700
And in fact, this would lead to higher levels of employment,

139
01:85:03,700 --> 01:85:06,820
lower unemployment, more rapid recoveries and recessions,

140
01:85:06,820 --> 01:85:08,260
but we'll talk about that more when we get

141
01:85:08,260 --> 01:85:10,120
to the labor economics section.

142
01:85:11,720 --> 01:85:13,380
All right, questions, questions.

143
01:85:13,380 --> 01:85:15,620
Anyone bringing about anything?

144
01:85:15,620 --> 01:85:18,740
All right, then let's move on to a topic

145
01:85:18,740 --> 01:85:21,740
that I have researched and therefore super important.

146
01:85:21,740 --> 01:85:22,940
This is an endogenous, right?

147
01:85:22,940 --> 01:85:26,300
I wouldn't research it unless it's super important.

148
01:85:26,300 --> 01:85:30,060
All right, now, have you ever heard an economist say,

149
01:85:30,060 --> 01:85:32,420
preferences explain everything and therefore nothing,

150
01:85:32,420 --> 01:85:34,220
or something like that?

151
01:85:34,220 --> 01:85:36,620
All right, so, so you say, well, maybe what happened

