You can explain anything with preferences.

156
01:85:45,580 --> 01:85:46,500
And explain anything,

157
01:85:46,500 --> 01:85:48,780
they didn't really explain anything at all.

158
01:85:48,780 --> 01:85:51,380
All right, so this attitude all comes back

159
01:85:51,380 --> 01:85:55,020
to an article by Gary Becker and George Stigler,

160
01:85:55,020 --> 01:85:58,220
where they made an argument that is so odd,

161
01:85:58,220 --> 01:85:59,860
it really must be read to be believed,

162
01:85:59,860 --> 01:86:01,460
but I will just tell you what they said,

163
01:86:01,460 --> 01:86:04,260
and if you don't believe me, you can go and double check.

164
01:86:04,260 --> 01:86:07,820
So what they said is, constant preferences,

165
01:86:07,820 --> 01:86:09,980
or again, constant or also called staples,

166
01:86:09,980 --> 01:86:13,340
stable, constant preferences, that's not really enough.

167
01:86:13,340 --> 01:86:14,660
You know, and when it's not just preferences,

168
01:86:14,660 --> 01:86:17,020
that's not enough, you know what we need to assume?

169
01:86:17,020 --> 01:86:18,500
Identical preferences.

170
01:86:20,700 --> 01:86:21,580
See, well, what do you mean

171
01:86:21,580 --> 01:86:23,180
you're gonna assume identical preferences?

172
01:86:23,180 --> 01:86:25,060
We're gonna assume that all human beings

173
01:86:25,060 --> 01:86:27,700
throughout all human history in every country and society

174
01:86:27,700 --> 01:86:29,260
have wanted exactly the same things

175
01:86:29,260 --> 01:86:32,260
and have had exactly the same fundamental utility function.

176
01:86:33,500 --> 01:86:36,420
But they totally don't, right?

177
01:86:36,420 --> 01:86:40,060
Some people really like football and others don't, right?

178
01:86:40,060 --> 01:86:41,900
And this is where Becker and Stigler say,

179
01:86:41,900 --> 01:86:44,460
well, that's what a naive person would think,

180
01:86:44,460 --> 01:86:47,460
but the real story, the real story,

181
01:86:47,460 --> 01:86:50,260
is that football fans face different constraints

182
01:86:50,260 --> 01:86:53,980
than non-football fans, and that's what's going on.

183
01:86:53,980 --> 01:86:54,820
Like what?

184
01:86:54,820 --> 01:86:56,660
Well, I mean, they must have some kind

185
01:86:56,660 --> 01:86:58,820
of different price factors or income,

186
01:86:58,820 --> 01:87:01,100
a specialized income that they're having,

187
01:87:01,100 --> 01:87:03,220
or it may be that they had more time

188
01:87:03,220 --> 01:87:05,460
to build up a certain kind of human capital

189
01:87:05,460 --> 01:87:08,780
that allows them to appreciate football games,

190
01:87:08,780 --> 01:87:11,500
and then another person might have spent their life

191
01:87:11,500 --> 01:87:13,740
building up the human capital to appreciate books,

192
01:87:13,740 --> 01:87:15,860
but it's not that one person is a book person

193
01:87:15,860 --> 01:87:17,420
and another one's a sports person.

194
01:87:17,420 --> 01:87:19,700
It's that they had prior investments

195
01:87:19,700 --> 01:87:22,220
which went and changed their production function

196
01:87:22,220 --> 01:87:24,740
for utility, but fundamentally,

197
01:87:24,740 --> 01:87:26,380
everyone's got the same function.

198
01:87:27,860 --> 01:87:30,860
Now, I think they would be okay if you were to say,

199
01:87:30,860 --> 01:87:33,340
like some people are born smarter than others,

200
01:87:33,340 --> 01:87:36,260
and it's easy for a smart person to appreciate a book

201
01:87:36,260 --> 01:87:38,420
than for a not-so-smart person.

202
01:87:38,420 --> 01:87:39,860
I think they might be okay with that,

203
01:87:39,860 --> 01:87:42,100
because they say, ah, so then it's the people,

204
01:87:42,100 --> 01:87:44,180
everybody has the same utility function,

205
01:87:44,180 --> 01:87:46,020
which is everyone receives utility

206
01:87:46,020 --> 01:87:48,860
from understanding books that they read,

207
01:87:48,860 --> 01:87:50,780
and then smart people can get the utility

208
01:87:50,780 --> 01:87:52,700
because they're able to understand the books,

209
01:87:52,700 --> 01:87:54,900
and not-so-smart people can't get utility from that

210
01:87:54,900 --> 01:87:56,740
because they can't understand the books,

211
01:87:56,740 --> 01:87:58,300
so then it's the same utility function

212
01:87:58,300 --> 01:88:01,380
but a different constraint and the theory of sound,

213
01:88:01,380 --> 01:88:03,300
and that's the way they put it,

214
01:88:03,300 --> 01:88:05,300
and I think they actually, yes,

215
01:88:05,300 --> 01:88:09,020
so I think this might be a footnote

216
01:88:09,020 --> 01:88:10,500
where they talk about things like,

217
01:88:10,500 --> 01:88:12,100
well, what about Jews keeping kosher?

218
01:88:12,100 --> 01:88:14,900
Doesn't that just mean that Jews like keeping kosher?

219
01:88:14,900 --> 01:88:18,140
And they say, well, that's not good enough, really,

220
01:88:18,140 --> 01:88:21,620
because, well, first of all, they don't have to be Jews.

221
01:88:23,580 --> 01:88:26,100
So you may say it's part of being part of that religion,

222
01:88:26,100 --> 01:88:28,020
but you could just say, fine,

223
01:88:28,020 --> 01:88:30,300
give me your theory about why they're in that religion.

224
01:88:30,300 --> 01:88:32,020
Say, well, their tastes were formed at a young age.

225
01:88:32,020 --> 01:88:33,460
No, no, no, no, no, no, no.

226
01:88:33,460 --> 01:88:35,340
No, you can't say that your tastes are formed

227
01:88:35,340 --> 01:88:37,080
by being raised in a religion.

228
01:88:37,080 --> 01:88:41,100
That's violating the identical preferences assumption.

229
01:88:41,100 --> 01:88:42,500
Now, if you were to go and reframe it

230
01:88:42,500 --> 01:88:46,540
and say, fine, they built up Jewish human capital,

231
01:88:46,540 --> 01:88:48,460
which other people don't build up,

232
01:88:48,460 --> 01:88:51,420
and so the fundamental utility function is not different,

233
01:88:51,420 --> 01:88:53,420
that's okay, although even there,

234
01:88:53,420 --> 01:88:56,460
they'd be, wait, it sounds like you're making fun of me now.

235
01:88:56,460 --> 01:88:59,660
But any case, Stigler and Becker

236
01:88:59,660 --> 01:89:02,060
were enormously influential with this work,

237
01:89:02,060 --> 01:89:03,900
and even economists who have no idea

238
01:89:03,900 --> 01:89:05,100
where this idea came from,

239
01:89:05,100 --> 01:89:07,620
or often are themselves, whenever you say, well,

240
01:89:07,620 --> 01:89:10,100
here's my theory about why people are doing it.

241
01:89:10,100 --> 01:89:11,580
The people who are doing it like it,

242
01:89:11,580 --> 01:89:13,340
and the people who aren't doing it don't like it.

243
01:89:13,340 --> 01:89:15,980
Well, there's nothing else for economics to say.

244
01:89:15,980 --> 01:89:18,540
Why is it that some people are eating the chocolate ice cream

245
01:89:18,540 --> 01:89:20,140
and some are eating the vanilla?

246
01:89:21,340 --> 01:89:24,420
And it's the same price for all the ice cream.

247
01:89:24,420 --> 01:89:26,460
Some people like chocolate, some like vanilla.

248
01:89:26,460 --> 01:89:27,700
It's in the utility function.

249
01:89:27,700 --> 01:89:29,540
There's nothing more to say,

250
01:89:29,540 --> 01:89:32,460
and yet I've heard economists say, oh, no, no, no, no.

251
01:89:32,460 --> 01:89:34,280
You can explain anything that way.

252
01:89:34,280 --> 01:89:36,220
Like then if you saw someone drinking motor oil,

253
01:89:36,220 --> 01:89:38,220
you could say, well, he likes motor oil.

254
01:89:39,340 --> 01:89:40,860
So, no, no, no.

255
01:89:40,860 --> 01:89:43,380
There's gotta be a better explanation

256
01:89:43,380 --> 01:89:45,620
for why some people are eating chocolate ice cream

257
01:89:45,620 --> 01:89:47,060
and others are eating vanilla.

258
01:89:48,060 --> 01:89:49,820
And again, the story, and again, it comes down to,

259
01:89:49,820 --> 01:89:51,580
it's not really, what you're doing isn't science

260
01:89:51,580 --> 01:89:53,140
because it explains everything.

261
01:89:54,300 --> 01:89:57,620
All right, now, what I see in response is

262
01:89:57,620 --> 01:89:59,580
there's actually a lot of empirical evidence

263
01:89:59,580 --> 01:90:00,980
that's just not true.

264
01:90:00,980 --> 01:90:02,940
So I have a paper in the Journal of Economic Behavior

265
01:90:02,940 --> 01:90:05,380
and Organization on personality economics.

266
01:90:05,380 --> 01:90:06,780
Now, personality is an area

267
01:90:06,780 --> 01:90:09,060
that almost no economists have studied.

268
01:90:09,060 --> 01:90:11,220
At the time that I wrote, very few had.

269
01:90:11,220 --> 01:90:13,620
Since I did it, James Heckman got interested,

270
01:90:13,620 --> 01:90:14,980
and so he's done a bunch of papers,

271
01:90:14,980 --> 01:90:16,900
and his co-authors have done a bunch of papers.

272
01:90:16,900 --> 01:90:18,940
It's still a very small number.

273
01:90:18,940 --> 01:90:22,400
But anyway, in this paper, I go over a whole lot of evidence

274
01:90:22,400 --> 01:90:25,060
from personality psychology that indicates

275
01:90:25,060 --> 01:90:26,900
that there is a high degree of preference heterogeneity.

276
01:90:26,900 --> 01:90:29,180
Human beings don't have identical preferences.

277
01:90:29,180 --> 01:90:32,300
For example, the reason why some people are librarians

278
01:90:32,300 --> 01:90:33,820
and other people are salesmen

279
01:90:33,820 --> 01:90:36,740
isn't that some people just randomly started acquiring

280
01:90:36,740 --> 01:90:38,380
librarian human capital

281
01:90:38,380 --> 01:90:39,540
and others started randomly acquiring

282
01:90:39,540 --> 01:90:40,980
salesman human capital.

283
01:90:40,980 --> 01:90:44,820
Instead, some people were born very introverted,

284
01:90:44,820 --> 01:90:46,780
some people were born very extroverted,

285
01:90:46,780 --> 01:90:49,660
and if this is the way that you were born,

286
01:90:49,660 --> 01:90:51,220
well, if you were born as an introvert,

287
01:90:51,220 --> 01:90:53,300
being a librarian sounds like a much better job

288
01:90:53,300 --> 01:90:54,140
than being a salesman,

289
01:90:54,140 --> 01:90:55,260
where if you were born extroverted,

290
01:90:55,260 --> 01:90:56,940
being a salesman seems like a much better job

291
01:90:56,940 --> 01:90:58,740
than being a librarian.

292
01:90:58,740 --> 01:91:00,900
Right, and lo and behold, we can use personality

293
01:91:00,900 --> 01:91:03,660
to go and predict occupational choice very well, actually.

294
01:91:03,660 --> 01:91:06,460
It's one of the best predictors of occupational choice

295
01:91:06,460 --> 01:91:08,320
is what your personality is.

296
01:91:08,320 --> 01:91:09,900
Almost every personality element

297
01:91:09,900 --> 01:91:12,780
has some predictable occupational profile.

298
01:91:12,780 --> 01:91:16,580
Like, you can think about the profile of a typical economist.

